# TK Shock: a music video built entirely from chat

**J directs, Claude builds.** J opens Unity, plays, takes notes, and gives direction. J never does work in Unity: no menus,
no scene edits, no running commands, no importing. If a step would need J's hands, automate it instead. If something
can't be done, build what does it. When something seems done, take it a step further and look at frames before saying so.

Read `Director/TREATMENT.md` (J's vision, keep adding to it) and `Director/SONG.md` (the song, bar by bar) before directing.

## The loop

1. J plays in Unity (Music Video desk window: Space plays, M or F8 marks, type a note) and writes direction in chat.
2. `node Director/mv.mjs marks` gives J's marks: time, bar:beat, section, note, screenshot.
3. Edit `Director/acts/*.json`, then `node Director/mv.mjs build`. It compiles the code too and reports errors.
4. Look: `mv frame 12:3`, `mv sheet 25:1 33:1 --every 3`. Read the PNG. Never ship what you haven't looked at.
5. Tell J what changed and where to look.

**Review Room:** J also watches renders at http://127.0.0.1:8795 (`room` in `.claude/launch.json`, `Director/room/server.mjs`) and clicks a moment to leave a note. Notes are in `Director/videos/<name>/notes.json`, and Unity marks arrive there too. Work them with `node Director/room/next.mjs`: no argument shows the next open note as JSON with its frame, shot and J's words, `take` also marks it in progress, `done <id> <clip> [--note "what changed"]` marks it fixed with a shot render or a whole new render (it makes the before and after clips), `ask <id> "<question>"` puts it to J, `list` shows them all.

**The note loop** (`Director/room/auto.mjs`, started by the Room's server; nobody runs it). On a video made of cards, a note with words runs by itself: Claude (Sonnet through Hawkes Gen) turns J's words and the frame into the smallest tweak to that shot's card (`videos/<v>/tweaks/zz-room.json`, never a card or an act), `cards compile`, a plain `build`, `mv shot` of that shot, the before and after clips beside the note, QA on the shot (`tools/qa.mjs --strict`), then the 30 fps review of the shot render (`tools/review.mjs` with `offset`, report in `review/note-<id>/`). Every step shows on the note in the Room (`auto.step`), and the QA score and the review's A/B/C count stay on the card. A note that fails QA or has an A fault stays open as "needs J" with the reason, the change still in, and the Room's Undo takes it out; one too vague to act on is left open with a question. Proven on greener v9 (2026-11-07): a framing note went note to QA verdict in 87 s (build 33 s, render 10 s) and was left open because QA scored the wider lens 5.9; a timing note went note to fixed in 84 s (build 26 s, QA 7.4, review 0 A); "this looks bad" was left open asking what bothers J. Vision costs about $0.05 a note.

The score is the source of truth. The scene's `_Generated` and every Timeline track are rebuilt from it on each build, so
never hand-edit them. Hand changes in Unity are lost; put them in the score.

## Commands (Unity may be in the background; it only needs to be open)

`node Director/mv.mjs` `ping | status | build | marks | marks-clear | open | setup | refresh`,
`frame <times>`, `sheet <from> <to> --every <beats> --cols N`, `seek|play <time>`, `pause`,
`render [--from --to --w --h --fps]` (final mp4 in `Director/output/`),
`find <words> [--type model|prefab|audio|texture|animation|material|vfx]`, `pull "<package>" "<path words>"...`,
`call peek '{"pos":[x,y,z],"look":[x,y,z]}'` (free camera on the set), `call look '{"t":100}'` (every look value at a moment),
`call feedrt` (dumps what the corner camera sends the screens, plus each TV's block), `call inspect '{"path":"Assets/..."}'` (size, rig, parts), `call clips '{"path":"..."}'`, `call bench '{"t":[100]}'` (frame cost).

`node Director/tools/reverse_test.mjs` proves frames match forward and backward. Run it after engine changes. Bars 58-68 can fail it on grain alone (heavy `scan` and `grain`): look at the `_a` and `_b` frames before believing it.

**QA:** `node Director/tools/qa.mjs <shot|all>` checks rendered shots before J sees them. A shot is a number, a word of its name, a time (`32.5`, `12:3`) or a range (`32-34`). It reviews the shot's own `mv shot` render when that is newer than the last full render, otherwise that part of the full render, and needs no Unity except for motion check. Each shot gets:
1. Stepped sheets of 12 frames per 3 s, and a burst of consecutive 30 fps frames at its busiest moments, with times burned in (`videos/<name>/shots/<id>/sheet.jpg`, `burst.jpg`).
2. Measurements of the render at 160x90: `flipFrames` (a body jumps to another pose and back, which only 30 fps shows; lamp flicker reads the same, so the reviewer has to confirm it), `pops`, darkness, white-out, a rough subject size, and the cut's distance from the beat grid.
3. `mv motioncheck` for the shot's time, only if Unity is free, otherwise it uses the last report if that is newer than the render.
4. Claude Vision through Hawkes Gen (`llm.json`, project `tkshock`, Sonnet at medium effort, about $0.025 a shot) with `review/STANDARD.md`, the card and the measurements: faults with times, severity and a fix.
5. `shots/<id>/qa.json` with a score out of 10. An A fault seen for certain, or a score under 7, fails the shot.

`--strict` exits 2 if any shot fails: the gate. `--notes` sends each failing shot's certain A and B faults to the Review Room as notes with source `qa`, which `room/next.mjs` hands out; they sit in the Room's QA tab, not in J's Open list, and a later pass closes them. `shots/qa-report.md` and `.json` rank every shot worst first (`qa.mjs report` rewrites them), and failed shots get a red line on the Room's timeline. `--render f.mp4 --label x` reviews an old render, cut by detected cuts, into `captures/qa/x/`. An unchanged shot costs nothing: the answer is kept against a hash of the sheets and prompt; `--force` asks again. `--no-vision` is measurements only. Story needs a real card: without an `idea`, story faults can't fail a shot. Calibration, catch rate and what it can't see: `Director/review/qa-calibration.md`; rerun with `node Director/tools/qa-calibrate.mjs <label> --truth v9`. `qa.mjs selftest` proves the measuring.

**Build tests:** `mv test` runs `reverse` (eight moments match going backward, 30 dB after a soft blur, so grain doesn't count), `size` (scene under 50 MB, timeline under 30, Generated under 400) and `cost` (ms per frame at six moments, 25% plus 15 ms over `<video>/test-baseline.json` fails; `--set-baseline` accepts new numbers). `--only cost` picks one. A plain `build` runs `size`. Results: `captures/test-report.json`.

**Arms:** gestures are mocap clips on `layer`s first. Use `reach` only for real contact. Stacked `reach` targets lock elbows and bend wrists (J, 2026-11-05).

`reach` and `plant`: the hand now follows the forearm by default, so wrists stay natural. A hand `reach` swings round the shoulder, and the forearm trails the upper arm by a frame. `"ease":"hand"` is the natural move: smooth off the mark, a little past it, and back. `"freeWrist":0` keeps the hand's world rotation, `handRot` sets it.

**Camera:** a plain `build` gives every shot that looks at a body a baked dead zone, about a fifth of the frame, so the camera doesn't twitch with the subject. `"deadzone":false` on a shot turns it off. `--fast` skips it.

`build --fast` is for test builds: the timeline stays in memory, nothing is saved, and it reuses what hasn't changed (see Toolchain below). Before J plays or a full render, do a plain `build`.

After a C# edit, `build` can report the previous compile errors or run on the old code: `refresh`, wait for the reload, then `build`.

Asset Store: `find`/`pull` work on J's downloaded `.unitypackage` files (index in `Director/assets/index.json`, rebuild with
`py Director/tools/index_assets.py`). `pull` takes only what's needed plus what it references, skips pack scripts, and converts
materials to `MV/Lit`. Purchases that were never downloaded are not on disk; don't scrape the Store, Unity's terms forbid it.

## Toolchain: many videos, lint, shots, queue

**Videos.** `node Director/mv.mjs --video <name> <command>` picks one; without it, `Director/current-video` (`mv use <name>`), then `tkshock`. `mv videos` lists them. TK Shock stays where it always was: `Director/score.json`, `Director/acts/`, `Assets/Scenes/MusicVideo.unity`, `Assets/MusicVideo/Generated/`. Every other video is `Director/videos/<name>/` (`score.json`, `acts/`, `audio.*`, `song.json`, `marks.json`, `sfx-map.json`, `inserts/`, `captures/`, `shots/`) plus `Assets/Videos/<name>/` (`<name>.unity`, `Generated/`, `Song/`). `Assets/MusicVideo/` holds what all videos share: engine, Person, Turkey, Props, Motion, Sfx.

`mv new <name> --song <file>` makes the folders, copies the song, writes a starter score and act, and runs demucs and `analyze_deep.py` (`--quick` is the plain `analyze_song.py`, no stems, and can read double time). `--bpm 90 --offset 0` states the tempo for a click or placeholder: a straight grid, bar 1 on the first beat. `mv song <file> [--bpm --offset --quick]` swaps a video's song: lengths are in beats, so `mv cards` then reflows every shot and no card changes. In a score, `"sharedModels":"../../score.json"` is the link to the shared models: that score's `models`, with your own on top.

**Stale scenes.** `frame`, `sheet`, `shot`, `render`, `previs` and `cards sheet` refuse when the last build failed or its inputs have changed since, and name the files (`Director/gen/build-<video>.json`). `--allow-stale` overrides. C# changes aren't covered.

**Unused keys.** `mv lint` and `mv cards check` warn, with file, line and a fix, on a key nothing uses: `tools/mv/keyuse.mjs`. `--strict` makes them errors. A key read in shared code for every kind of cue still passes.

**Lint.** `mv lint [--strict --all --limit N --reindex]`, in Node, about a second. It runs before every `build` and stops it on an error, printing file, line and a plain fix. It checks every trap in `Director/PIPELINE.md` that shows in text, plus unknown cue keys (read from the C#, so the list can't go stale), unknown clips (the `.fbx.meta` files), `look` arrays, times outside the song, `move`/`rotate` on humans, duplicate actors, sets with no `from`/`until`, lights with no end, a hand or foot target with no prop, cuts and text per bar against `"targets":{"cutsPerBar":{"s5":3,"DROP":4}}` in the score, a scene over 50 MB, and on native humans the clip catalog (`clip-banned`, `clip-weak-use`, `clip-needs-grip`, see **The clip catalog**). Warnings become errors with `--strict`. Deliberate exceptions go on the cue or actor: `"lint":"slide"` (move on a human), `"lint":"point"` (a reach into air), `"lint":"always"` (a set or directional light on all the time). `lint-baseline.json` in the video's folder holds accepted findings, so a finished video builds and only new problems stop it: `mv lint --baseline` writes it, `--all` shows them. Keep it empty on a new video.

**Shots.** `mv shots` lists shots with bar, beat, length and name. `mv shot <n|name> [--handle 1 --final]` renders one at 640x360, 30 fps, with sound and a 1 s handle each side, the same picture as a final (depth of field and every look key) with only edge smoothing off (`--final` keeps it), to `Director/videos/<name>/shots/`, and writes a contact sheet beside it. `mv previs [--fps 12]` renders the whole video at 426x240 with no post, AO, shadows or Stylize pass, and the TV feed, eye and doors only every 4th frame, to `Director/videos/<name>/previs.mp4`. **Previs plays the baked takes**: the build picks a bake by key whenever one exists (`MVBake.cs`), at every quality, so motion is judged from the first rough cut; `previs` prints how many takes played baked and warns, by name, when one played from source (`mv bake` first). `mv shot` renders the picture only and muxes the sound in Node (`tools/mv/sound.mjs`, the same mix as a final's), so every frame is kept: Transport.cs's `-shortest` mux dropped a shot's last 2 frames.

**Builds.** A plain `mv build` makes everything and saves. `mv build --fast` is for working: it keeps the actors when `actors`, `models`, `stage` and `shows` are unchanged, and keeps every track whose clips are unchanged, so a cue edit is quick. The first `--fast` after a plain build, or after any C# change, builds everything once (reuse lives in statics, so new code can't run on old objects). `--fast --full` forces that. Dead zones come from the last plain build when the score is identical; otherwise they are off until the next plain build.

**Bake gate.** Every `mv cards` and `mv build` first bakes every human take the cut uses whose bake is missing (`tools/bake/key.mjs` computes the compiler's own bake key in Node, so no Unity is needed to know; `--no-bake` skips, and the takes play from source), then builds, then writes `videos/<v>/bake-report.json`: each take use's baked numbers, judged against `Director/library/bake-gate.json` (per video: snaps 5, acceleration spikes 0, elbow and knee flips 0, hyperextension 0; per take: ankle over range 200 frames, near-floor foot travel 2.5 m; set so Greener v9 passes, tighten as the bake improves). `mv final` and `mv render` refuse on a failing report and name the take and the number; `--allow-bake` renders anyway. `mv bake status` prints the report; `mv bake` bakes what is stale, `mv bake <take...>` named takes (`tools/bake.mjs --stale`).

**Review.** `mv review [<video>] [render.mp4] [--label v9 --no-vision --force]` is the 30 fps reviewer as a tool (`tools/review.mjs`, Node and ffmpeg, no Unity): every frame measured at 160x90 (frame difference, a came-back test, block-matching flow with the camera's shift taken out), then a strip of 8 consecutive frames at every cut and every spike, cropped on him (the compiled shot's lens and subject, the motion's centre; a centre crop otherwise), and Claude vision through Hawkes Gen (`llm.json`, project `tkshock`, Sonnet) on each shot's strips with `review/STANDARD.md`. `videos/<v>/review/<label>/report.md` lists A/B/C faults with time, frame, shot and fix, as `videos/greener/v5-review.md` did by hand; `report.json` beside it, strips in `shots/<id>/`. Answers are kept against a hash of the strips and prompt, so an unchanged shot costs nothing; about $1.50 a video. **`mv final` runs it on the finished file and prints the A faults** (`--no-review` skips); a version with an A fault is not one for J. Nobody calls a render good from a contact sheet. `node Director/tools/review.mjs notes <video> <label> [--sevs A,B]` turns a report's certain A faults into Review Room notes with source `review` (the Room's QA tab, never J's Open list, never the note loop), once each, and closes the ones a later report of the same label no longer has; the 18 A faults of greener v9 are there. `reviewRender(V, file, { offset })` reviews a shot render against the cards it overlaps, with fault times in video seconds; the note loop uses it. **Re-reviews are incremental** (`tools/mv/verdicts.mjs`, 2026-11-07): `mv final` writes the shot hashes of every file it makes to `final/renders/<file>.json`, and a review of that file keys each shot's verdict by the final shot hashes its frames cover (its own, and the shot before for the cut strip), the card's idea and feel, `review/STANDARD.md` and the model, in `videos/<v>/review/verdicts.json`. A shot whose key is there keeps that verdict with the label it came from (no strips, no Claude, $0; `carried` in the report); only re-rendered shots and the shot after each are asked. `--force` asks every shot; `mv review --plan` lists what would be asked and carried and spends nothing; `node Director/tools/review.mjs adopt <video> <label>` keeps an earlier review of a final's render by its hashes; `review.mjs test` is the self test.

**Freeze.** `mv freeze [--note "..."]` records a hash of the engine (Runtime C# and shaders, as `shotdeps.engineHash`; on a video with any native actor also the native compile, `Editor/Native/NativeCompile.cs`, `MVClipClean.cs`, `MVClipImport.cs`; `Runtime/MVNativeActing.cs` is Runtime already), every C# file under `Assets/MusicVideo`, and his model (`Assets/MusicVideo/Person`) in `videos/<v>/freeze.json`; `mv thaw` lifts it, `mv freeze status` compares. While frozen, `mv final --plan` warns in red when the engine or the model moved since (every shot would re-render) and `mv cards` warns on any C# change. The rule: engine and model changes before the slice, frozen after, so a fix costs minutes, not a full render. **A shot's key covers what it plays** (2026-11-07): besides its cues, actors and model files, each clip its actors play (their cues and the performances they run: `clip`, `from: "clip@t"`, `blendTree` rows, a performance's clip args) is a part of its own, `clip <name>`, stamped from the clip's file, its `.meta` (import settings), its entry in `library/motion/import.json` (loop, root motion, Foot IK, `clean.use`) and, for a native actor, its clean copy in `Motion/Clean` (`tools/mv/clipfiles.mjs`); materials and textures an actor names outside its model's folder are `files <actor>`. So a reimported or re-cleaned clip re-renders only the shots that play it, and `--plan` names it ("changed: clip mx_sit_idle"). A render saved before this renders once ("saved before the shot key covered clips"). `final --plan` compiles the cards in memory first (it writes nothing, not even the act), so it answers for the cards as they are now, and also prints how many review verdicts will carry and what the asked ones cost. `final --plan` also prints why each shot renders, one line each, and a summary: shots and frames reused, the reasons, and the minutes at the last final's pace. **A real `mv final` says up front how long it expects**, before it waits for Unity: shots and frames to render, the seconds a frame from the last final's rendered shots, and that final's build, stitch and review time on top (`final/progress.json` carries it as `expected`, for the Room).

**The scene file** holds the camera, the director and little else. Everything under `_Generated` is left out at save, and a video's scene that opens without it builds itself from the score. So don't expect `_Generated` in the file, and never hand-edit it. The timeline of a `--fast` build lives in memory (`HideFlags.DontSave`); every build destroys the previous one, along with the director that held bindings to it. A plain build fails if the scene passes 50 MB, and lint reports it too.

**Unity queue.** Every `mv` call that touches Unity takes a ticket in `Director/gen/queue/` and waits its turn, first come first served. A ticket goes when its process goes, however it dies, and one that stops beating for 2 minutes is dropped, so nothing is left stale. `mv queue` shows who is running and waiting. `mv hold <command line>` keeps the turn across several calls: `mv hold node Director/tools/reverse_test.mjs`. **One Unity on this laptop.** A second render lane was measured on 2026-11-07 and is not worth it here: the editor is 5.9 GB working set and 9.7 GB commit on a 15.8 GB machine with 1 GB free, and a second editor on a project copy would page it out and reach the commit level that crashed Greener twice (PIPELINE.md "Speed"). Use the queue; a second lane means a second machine. **Never make `Director/gen/unity.lock` by hand.** The queue writes it for old scripts, and waits 90 s for a hand-made one before stopping with a message. `render` now holds the queue to the last frame, and `--lock` on `motioncheck` is ignored.

**Motion check:** bodies wholly behind something the camera can see are not reported: rays from the camera to each body part, against the real meshes, skipping transparent and skinned ones. `mv motioncheck --shot <n|name>` checks one shot. Each run is kept in `captures/motioncheck-runs/`, with `latest-<range>.json` and `.md` for each range (`latest-all` is the whole cut; `motioncheck.json` is only the last run), and compared with the last run of the same range (or the pinned one, `--set-baseline`), printing `new`, `fixed`, `same`.

**Shot checks** (camera and staging, 2026-11-07; on the Hawkes Punch slice 8 of 11 faults were these, not animation). Six more checks inside the same walk, on samples only, ranked with the rest in `motioncheck.json` and the report, each with a level: **A stops a render**, B is reported. They read the cards through `captures/shotspec.json`, which `mv` writes before every motion check (`tools/mv/shotspec.mjs`: whose shot it is, each cast actor's mark and clip, and each clip's measured hips height from `library/clips.json`); a video without cards takes `person` as the subject and skips mark. A fault "lasts" when it covers 0.3 s or 35% of its shot.
- **frame**: the subject (the card camera's `on:` actor, else the first human in the cast) with both eyes in frame (A when lasting), the face not cut 30% (B) or 40% (A, lasting) at the top or a side, eyes not in the top 7% (B), and, when the frame holds at least a metre at the head, the body not 35% (B) or 50% (A) out at a side; a named subject wholly out of frame is A. Other actors facing the camera get the face and body edge checks as B ("(not the subject)"). Off for `extreme-close-up` (`ecu`, a new preset: lens 100, frame 0.22) or the `ecu` tag, or when the frame is under 30 cm tall at the head; a bone subject (`on: person.hips`) or the `insert` tag only asks that the bone is in frame.
- **gesture**: a hand or foot whose speed over the hips' peaks above 1.2 m/s mid-shot (0.15 s from either cut, hips under 0.6 m/s) while it is outside the frame by up to one frame height and the head or chest is in it. B.
- **mark**: a cast human's root more than 30 cm from its card mark (or the hips 75 cm), A at 60 cm lasting; standing where the card's clip or the playing clip sits (hips 15 cm over the clip's measured top; 0.65 m on a seat mark with no measured clip), A when lasting. Only actors on camera, not during walk, fly, jump, ragdoll, throws, keys or root motion, not attached, not with the `offmark` tag.
- **readable**: one 160x90 render of the shot's camera at its middle; the subject's pixels (its capsules projected) against a ring around them, the larger of the luminance step and 0.6 of the colour distance: under 0.07 B, under 0.035 A. `dark` on a card skips it. Every probed shot's number is in `shotcheck.json` (`contrast`); `--probes` keeps the frames in `captures/shotcheck-probes/`, and a flagged one is always kept.
- **camin**: the camera's near plane (centre or a corner) inside any body capsule, the head 2.5 cm wider: always A. A POV (camera at the eyes, looking the head's way) and the `pov` tag are allowed.
- **propat**: a prop `attach`ed to a bone other than a hand more than 3 cm from that bone's pose plus its `pos` (A at 8 cm lasting); a prop parented to a body's root that sits within 30 cm of the head, neck, chest or a hand when first seen, drifting more than 3 cm from that bone (glasses riding her root while she leans). Hands stay with `hand`.

`mv shotcheck [<video>] [--from --to | --shot n --probes]` runs only these (`motioncheck --checks shot`, samples every 1/15 s, nothing between): measured 10 to 18 s per minute of video in the clinic and about 40 s a minute over all of Hawkes Punch (space is heavier), nearly all of it Unity evaluating the timeline; the checks themselves are about 2 s a minute. A full `mv motioncheck` now includes them for about 2 s a minute more. `--checks` takes `joint`, `shot`, `contact` or a list. Card tags for deliberate shots: `tags: [ecu]`, `insert`, `pov`, `dark`, `offmark`. Each run is kept in `captures/shotcheck.json` with the hash of the inputs it ran on. **The shot gate:** `mv shot`, `mv render` (so slice renders) and `mv final` check what they render first (the kept report when the inputs haven't changed, else a fresh check) and refuse on an A fault, naming shot, actor, fault and time; `--allow-shot` renders anyway (`--force` too on `shot` and `render`; on `final` `--force` still means render every shot). `mv cards` ends with one line, `shot check: 2 A, 5 B (frame 3, mark 2...)`, from a 5 Hz pass over the whole cut (`--no-shotcheck` skips it); the gates reuse it while the inputs are unchanged. Proof and calibration: `videos/_shotcheck_test` (one bad card per fault class beside a good one; not a video for J). **Native bodies and what waits for the Animator** (`Runtime/MVLate.cs`, 2026-11-08). Inside a director Evaluate our tracks run before Unity's Animator writes a native body's pose (measured with `call nativeorder`, a probe output in the real graph). So an `attach` (and `catch`, a throw's target) on a native body read the last evaluation's bones: in a render the can trailed her hand by a frame and, on the first frame after a cut, sat where the hand was in the shot before (the 0.42 m in Hawkes Punch's d_can); and native `reach`, `gaze`, `eyes` and grips were written onto bones the Animator then replaced. Now, during an Evaluate, a native body's acting and every driver that reads a native bone are queued, and run on the finished pose, at the director's own time; a native body read at another time while they run (a gaze reading where its target will be, a lead camera reading ahead) is read into a stand-in and put straight back, and a last pass puts back anything still left at another time (Hawkes Punch w_out: `other` sat on the next card's mark for the last 0.3 s): before every render (`beginContextRendering`) and right after the Evaluates whose result is read without one (`mv frame`, renders, seek, the motion check, the dead-zone bake, the probes). `call attachprobe` reads 0 cm stepped from the frame before, jumped from 6 s away, and on the cut frame (was 10, 94 and 42 cm); evaluating the same time twice gives the same pose. Legacy (non-native) videos are untouched. A native video looks different after this: props stay in the hands, and the cards' native `look`, reach and fingers now reach the body. Between samples a native body is now evaluated with the whole timeline (Driver.Eval alone left its bones a sample old and laid gaze on top again): on Hawkes Punch 50-75 s that took pop-rot from 39 ranges to 13 and pop-move from 13 to 5, the same scene; a full check costs more on native videos. `call camprobe '{"t":1.47}'` compares the walk's camera with `mv frame`'s. **Broadcast text and the lens:** the graphics canvas is a plane in front of the lens; TMP text on it stops drawing when plane distance times tan(fov/2) is under about 0.05 (measured with `call gfxvariants`: at the old fixed 0.12 m every lower third in Hawkes Punch's tv_doc, 65 mm, drew its bar and lost its text). The plane now follows the lens before every render and gfx evaluation (`MVGfx.FitCanvases`: 0.08 / tan(fov/2), never under 0.12 m), so anything nearer the lens than that (under half a metre below 80 mm) covers the graphics, as on a real lens. `call gfxprobe '{"t":170}'` dumps every visible text's mesh and material with frames.

## Shot cards

New videos are written as cards, not cues. A card is one shot in a few lines; `Director/tools/cards.mjs` (Node, no Unity) compiles them to a normal act, `acts/50-cards.json`, which is generated: never edit it. Same input, same output. Cue-level detail is for special moments, in `raw`.

`mv cards sheet [--only a,b --at 0.1,0.5,0.9]` is the staging pass: three labelled frames per card, about 30 s, no render. `check` warns when a walk rounds to whole strides and would snap on a cut in view: space the marks as it says. `mv cards` compiles and builds (`--fast` as for `build`). `mv cards check` validates and prints the cut, `mv cards compile` writes the act, `mv cards note <card> <key> <value>` adds to J's notes, `mv why <card> <key>` says which layer set a value. All take `--video`. `node Director/tools/cards.mjs test` is the self test.

```
videos/<v>/cut.json        order and length, and nothing else holds them
           cards/*.yaml    the cards (or JSON)
           style.json      the whole video ("inherit": a file in library/styles/)
           sequences/*.json overrides for a part; a cut entry names one with "seq"
           tweaks/*.json   agents' fixes, keyed by card id
           notes/*.json    J's notes, keyed by card id. Strongest layer. No tool regenerates them
```

**Cut:** `{"beats":{"drop":"25:1"},"shots":[{"id":"alone","seq":"room","len":"4b"},{"id":"out","at":"drop","len":"2bars","take":"a"}]}`. Reorder, trim or drop a line and what follows moves. A shot without `at` follows the one before; `at` pins it to `12`, `12:3`, `41.2s`, a section or a name in `beats`, so it stays put; `after` names the shot it follows. Running past a pin trims with a warning. `len` or `at` anywhere else is an error. A pin may carry an offset: `"at":"punch-1b"`, `"at":"ask_done+0.4s"` with `"beats":{"ask_done":"22.84s"}`, so a cut on a joke sits a fixed beat of silence after a line however the line moves. `len` takes seconds as well as beats and bars (`"0.98s"`), and `mv cards insert/remove` keep it in its unit.

**The clock** (`tools/mv/clockshift.mjs`, 2026-11-07). `mv cards insert [<video>] <at> <length>` makes room in a video's clock and `mv cards remove [<video>] <at> <length>` takes time out; `<at>` is `12`, `12:3`, `41.2s`, a section, a cut name or `end`, `<length>` `8bars`, `32b` or `20s` (rounded to whole beats of the local spacing). Every absolute time at or after the point moves by the same amount, bar:beat strings by the same beats: song.json (new beats at the local spacing, duration, sections, bars, onsets, phrases, zeros in the envelopes), cut.json (pins and `beats` names; a shot over the point grows, the first shot after a gap is pinned so the cut stays with the acts), score.json and every act (each cue's `at`, `until`, `land`, absolute `sfxAt` and `keys` `t`, actors' `from`/`until`, sections; edited as text so comments stay), lyrics.json, lyrics-raw.json, duck.json, marks.json, notes.json (Room notes: t, at, bar, beat, frame), sfx-map.json, `lines.json`, and the audio (the score's song, `audio.*`, `stems/`: silence spliced in or the range cut out). It stops, writing nothing, on anything it can't place: a file in the video folder it doesn't know, a tool under `Director/tools` that names the video and may hold its times, an act generated by a tool whose times live in its code (`tools/punch_lines.mjs` until `--export` moves its LINES to `videos/hawkespunch/lines.json`, which it then reads), a relative time (`+1b`) whose cue before is on the other side of the point, a time it can't read; and on remove, a cue or shot inside the range unless `--drop`. It warns on a cue whose `for` or a sound whose length runs over the point (a bed plays on unchanged). Before writing it compiles the cards in a scratch copy and checks every card lands where the map says. Then it writes `videos/<v>/_backup/<op>-<bar>-<beats>b-<time>/` (every file before, and `rebase.json`: the map and each change, file by file), the files, and compiles the cards. `--dry` prints all of it and writes nothing; `--accept <file>` passes a file it would refuse; `--no-audio`. `clockshift.mjs test` is the self test. Design for a full dialogue clock: `Director/docs/dialogue-clock.md`.

**Card:** under 10 lines. `idea` and `feel` are required: what the shot is for and what we feel.
```yaml
- id: his_face
  idea: nothing on it reaches him
  feel: numb
  set: living_room               # usually from the sequence
  cast: {person: sofa sit_idle, her: door>by_tv clip:WalkForwardScared}
  camera: close-up person
  lens: 85                       # mm; the preset picks one
  mood: cold
  light: tv_glow                 # the set's own by default
  events: [{on: kick, pulse: {zoom: 0.01}}, {on: 2b, who: person, do: blink}]
  use: {reaction_cut: {at: 0.5b}}
  raw: [{at: 1b, do: drop, on: living_room_table, floor: 0}]
  takes: {b: {camera: high-lonely person}}
  note: anything
```

- **Cast** drives everything about an actor, so a card never writes `hide`, `show` or `set`. In the cast: shown, placed on its mark. Not in the cast: hidden. The same performance on the same mark runs on across the cut instead of restarting. An entry is `mark performance`, or `clip:Name` for a stand-in or plain clip, `a>b clip:Name` walks between marks (`a>b walk_tired` passes `to` and `dist` to the performance), turning to the mark's heading on the cut after, `look:her` gazes. No mark means stay where you are. As a map: `at to perform clip look face then args`. Actors must exist in the video's `acts/` or `score.json`; `face` takes degrees or a mark.
- **Camera:** `camera: <preset> <subject> [second subject] [left|right]`, or a map with `preset on side angle dist height frame lens screen push shake dutch behind aperture`. Presets: `close-up medium wide two-shot over-shoulder low-hero high-lonely tracking`, and `spot` (`{preset: spot, from: corner_cam, on: her}`, a camera mark). Pure math from marks and headings: it stands off along his heading so the frame holds a set height at that lens, the subject on a third with the eyes high, looking into the frame. Inside a set's `bounds` it swaps sides, then comes closer and opens the lens; a walk too big to ride beside becomes a pan from the middle. No shake, no dutch, no move unless asked (`push: 0.2`, `shake: 0.2`). A subject can be a bone, `person.chest`, or a mark. Over-shoulder names the shoulder first. Anything else: `shot: {fov: 22}` on a card or a tweak overrides the compiled shot, key by key; `null` removes one.
- **Layers**, per key, later wins: `style`, `sequence`, `card`, `take`, `tweak`, `note`. Maps merge, lists replace, `null` removes. A tweak or note is any card field: `{"lamp":{"mood":"cold","shot":{"fov":30},"cast":{"her":{"look":"person"}}}}`. Recompiling never touches them. A tweak for a card that doesn't exist is an error.
- **Takes** stay on disk in `takes`. `mv cards --take both=b` builds take b for one run; `"take":"b"` in the cut keeps it. So an A/B is one flag and two short renders.
- **Events:** `on` is the trigger: `kick snare hat all` (hits inside the shot), `beat`, `bar`, `start`, or a time in the shot like `2b`, `end`, `end-1b`. `who` is the actor. The rest is a cue. `raw` is a list of plain cues for one-offs, with `at` measured in the shot; set pieces are `<set>_<piece>`.
- **Templates** replace per-part scripts: `Director/library/templates/<name>.mjs`, a function of parameters that returns `{shot, cues}`, with `about`, `params` and a `test`. `use` applies them, parameters are checked. Have: `crash_zoom rack_focus hero_reveal reaction_cut`.
- **Sets:** `Director/library/sets/<name>.json`: `origin` (keep sets apart), `bounds [xmin,zmin,xmax,zmax]`, `ceiling`, `light`, `solids` (`{"table":[xmin,zmin,xmax,zmax,height]}`, `check` warns when one blocks the view of the subject), `marks` and `actors`. A mark is `{pos, face, kind: stand|seat|door|camera|prop, head}`, or `[x,y,z,face]`. Pieces exist only while a card stands on the set. `center` is where a lighting preset is anchored.
- **Lighting:** `Director/library/lighting/<name>.json`: `sky` and `lights`, on for the run of cards that use it and off after. **Moods:** `Director/library/moods.json`, each a whole grade, so a cut never inherits the last one.
- **Perform:** a word after the mark is a `perform` cue with that name; it must be a file in `Director/library/performances`, or `check` stops. `args` on a cast entry are its parameters. Use `clip:` until one exists.
- `cards.map.json` lists each shot's id, idea, feel, times and take, for the Review Room.

A wrong name in any of this stops `check` and says what you might have meant. `mv new` names can't start with `_`, so `videos/_cards_test` was made by hand; it is the working example.

## Styles, formats, lighting (any format, any style)

**One line picks each, and no shot changes.** In a video's `style.json`: `{"inherit":"neon-noir","format":"social-15-vertical"}`, or `"style"` and `"format"` in `score.json` for a video without cards. `mv style <name>` and `mv format <name>` swap that line (no name lists them, `format` prints the beat sheet with times; `--length 30` scales it), then `mv cards`. The video's own `style.json` is stronger, key by key: `"grade":{"grain":0.1}`, `"sky"`, `"lens"`, `"steps"`, `"aspect":[4,5]`, `"length":20`.

- **Style** `Director/library/styles/<name>.json`. Have: `filmic-night soft-daylight stop-motion graphic-comic vhs-found-footage neon-noir`. Keys: `about`, `light` (a lighting preset, the default for every card), `grade` (any look key, on from time 0), `sky`, `steps` (hz, stepped animation for the whole video), `fps` (`mv render` default; stop motion is 24), `lens` (mm per camera preset: `{"close-up":85,"wide":24}`, plus `aperture` to fix it, `apertureScale` to scale the presets', `handheld`), `type` (defaults under every `text` cue: `color style size pos in out tilt`, `caps:1`), `cues` (plain cues at the head of the score, like the VHS date stamp), and any card field (`mood`, `camera`). A card or a sequence still wins. **Moods move a style, they don't replace it:** a mood's change from `base` is added to the style's grade.
- **Style sheets:** `node Director/mv.mjs hold node Director/tools/mv/stylesheets.mjs [--styles a,b --clips]` builds `videos/styletest` once per style (its shots never change) and writes `Director/library/styles/sheets/<style>.png`, `compare.png`, and with `--clips` a short `<style>.mp4` for judging steps. A new style isn't done until its sheet reads as a look of its own.
- **Lighting** `Director/library/lighting/<name>.json`, the rigs: `portrait_window golden_hour golden_morning overcast studio_3_point moonlight neon_rig tv_glow evening_lamp street_dusk`. A light is keyed to the set: `"at":"window"` a mark of the set plus `"offset"`, or `"pos"` from the set's `center` when the set has no such mark, so one rig fits every set. `"aim":"subject"` (the `subject` mark, else `center`, at chest height), another mark, or a point computes `rot`. Keys `key fill rim practical` by habit, and `sun` for the directional that does the work outdoors. Marks a rig looks for: `window`, `key`, `fill`, `rim`, `tv`, `practical`.
- **Format** `Director/library/formats/<name>.json`. Have: `music-video ad-30 social-15-vertical trailer short-film explainer`. Keys: `aspect`, `render`, `fps`, `length`, `pacing` (`avgSeconds`, `maxSeconds`, `shots`), `beats` (the beat sheet: `part`, `seconds` or `share`, `job`), `fovScale`. `mv cards check` warns when the cut misses the pacing or length. The aspect reaches `frame`, `sheet`, `shot`, `previs` and `render`, which all take `--aspect 9:16`; `--w`, `--h`, `--fps`, `--length` beat the file. `render` and `previs` stop at the format's `length`. A tall frame opens every lens by `fovScale` (1.3 by default), centres the subject instead of a third, and sizes type to its width. After changing a format, build with `--full`: a reused set keeps the old canvas.
- **Look keys from the library** (all 0 unless asked, in `grade` and `pulse`): `vhs` tape, `split` with `splitA` shadow and `splitB` highlight colours, `lift` milky blacks, `halation` film bleed, `paper` printed stock, `flicker` and `weave` frame-to-frame exposure and gate, `clay` plasticine that boils on each step, `inkWidth` line weight for `ink`, `aspect` a window inside the frame (1.333 for 4:3). Daylight keys, also 0 unless asked: `haze` air by depth, cool, warmer toward the sun; `veil` the sun's glare on the lens; `leak` a warm light leak from the side the sun is on; `dust` motes in the air, world-fixed and pure of time; `curve` a film curve over the tonemap that pulls neon greens to olive; `skin` light wrapped and reddened on warm albedos, in `MV/Lit`; `sunRim` sun on silhouettes, in `MV/Lit`. Lights that follow the camera, also in `MV/Lit`: `faceFill` a big soft shadowless source at the lens on warm albedos, so eye sockets never go black; `catch` a small softbox beside the lens seen only in wet surfaces (smoothness over .8, the eyes), so they are never dead. `haze`, `veil` and `dust` need a sky cue with `sun` and `sunDir`.
- **Sky** now also takes `sun`, `sunDir`, `sunSize`, `haze`, `clouds` (0-1 cover), `cloudColor`, `cloudShade`, `cloudScale`, `cloudSpeed`, `cloudSoft` (edge softness, 0.2 by default, 0.4 painterly), `stars`, and `ambientTop` with `ambientBottom` for light from above and below. All drawn from the view direction and scene time, so they scrub. Leave them out and it is the plain gradient.
- **Grass** an actor: `{"model":"none","grass":{"size":[17,12],"hero":[[x,z,r]],"push":"person.rightHand:0.1,person.rightFoot:0.1"}}`. Real blades (`MVGrass.cs`, `Shaders/MVGrass.shader`), thickest in the `hero` circles where the camera goes close, thinning to the edges. `height width seed quality heroDensity midDensity farDensity midRadius`, colours `dark mid tip dry soil`, `flowers` per square metre in drifts with `flowerA flowerB`, `exclude` boxes `[xmin,zmin,xmax,zmax]`, `wind windAngle windScale lean`. The wind is in the vertex shader on scene time, so it scrubs, reverses, and steps with `steps`. `push` names up to 8 bones or actors that part the blades, read fresh each frame. Each blade is a fixed-length arc, so nothing stretches. The meshes are built from these numbers and never saved in the scene. **Fence** an actor: `{"model":"none","fence":{"length":18,"height":1.75,"texture":"Assets/...","color":"#c89a6c"},"pos":..,"rot":..}`, dog-eared planks on the +z side, rails and posts behind.
- **Interiors and shadows:** a directional key has no position, so a ceiling blocks it. In a set, `"shell":true` on an actor (roof, walls, the house around a room) and `"shell":"room"` on the one whose bounds are the room (the ceiling): while a camera stands inside a room, all shells stop casting (`Runtime/MVShell.cs`, per camera render, no state). Outside, the house still shades the lawn. Rigged bodies now receive shadows by default; `"shadows":false` turns it off. Painted floors take their brightness down in the material (`hall_floor.mat`), or a key blows them out and the shadow can't read.
- **`boxUV`** on any actor, prim or model: tiles per metre. New UVs thrown along the mesh's faces at true size, whatever the scale, so a palette prop or a stretched cube wears a real texture: `{"model":"crate","boxUV":1.6,"texture":"Assets/...","color":"#ffffff","smooth":0.18}`, `tile` for a non-square map.
- **Sets outdoors:** `Director/library/sets/backyard.json`: lawn, fence, the back of the house, porch and back door. `sky` in a set goes over its lighting preset's sky. `bounds` are from the set's own `origin`.

## Keyed poses

Hand-keyed, pose-to-pose acting in Unity's own muscle space, so the native path plays exactly what was keyed (2026-11-08; research: `Director/research/epic-anime-action.md` 4 and 8, `zero-g-acting.md` 1). Use it for hero acting and the zero-g float; mocap and `gm_` clips are seed material.

- **`mv call posederive '{"sheet":"zg_neutral"}'`** (`Editor/Native/PoseDerive.cs`) reads `Director/library/poses/<name>.json` and writes `Assets/MusicVideo/Motion/Keyed/<name>.anim` (and `<name>_<variant>.anim` for each of `variants`; `"variant":"a"` one, `"variants":false` the base only). An ordinary humanoid clip: play it with `anim`/`layer` by name, on either path. Deterministic: no clock, no random. `"table":true` returns each key's resolved muscles.
- **A sheet:** `seed` `{clip, t}` (default `mx_breathing_idle@0`: every muscle and the body position/rotation), `poses`, `keys`, `loop`, `length`, `fps` (sample rate, 60), `step` + `stepFps` (the whole clip on 2s/3s), `mirror`, `add` (muscle offsets on every key), `fingers`, `root`, `cycles`, `cyclePhase`, `cycleScale`, `variants` (patches merged over the sheet: maps merge, lists replace, `null` removes), `inherit` (another sheet as the base). Comments allowed.
- **A pose:** `from` (another pose, or `{"clip":"mx_cross_punch","t":0.42}`: a frame of any clip, keyed or clean or source; its body stays where the seed has it unless `keepRoot`), `mirror`, `muscles` (absolute, any subset of the 95 `HumanTrait.MuscleName`s; the clip names `LeftHand.Index.1 Stretched` work too, and a wrong name stops with the nearest three), `add` (offsets), `fingers` (`{"both":{"curl":0.4,"spread":0}, "left.thumb":{"curl":0.2}, "right.index":{"joints":[0.5,0.4,0.3]}}`: curl 0 flat .. 1 fist, per finger beats per hand), `root` (`{"pos":[m],"rot":[deg]}` on the body).
- **A key:** `t`, `pose`, `interp` (how the move ARRIVES at this key, as legacy `keys`: `ease` (default), `linear`, `in`, `out`, `step` = hold the previous pose and snap at `t`, `overshoot` = past the mark by `overshoot` of the travel (default .1) and back over `settle` s (default .3)), `hold` (seconds on this pose before the next move starts), `step` (2 or 3: this move, its hold and settle sampled on 2s/3s at `stepFps` 24 and held; written as constant-tangent keys at the exact step times), plus per-key `muscles`, `add`, `fingers`, `root`, `mirror`. Times are seconds or `"6f"` (24 fps frames).
- **Cycles:** `{"period":4,"phase":0,"muscles":{"Chest Front-Back":0.03},"root":{"pos":[0,0.004,0],"rot":[1,2,0]}}` adds a sine (breath, float). On a loop each period must divide `length` (it warns). **Additive:** `"over":{"clip":"X","from":0,"speed":1}` makes the poses offsets on a playing clip (`clipderive` with poses).
- **Mirror** swaps Left/Right muscles, negates the midline Left-Right and twist muscles, and mirrors the body. **Limits:** muscles are clamped to -1..1 (the Avatar's range). Humanoid clips cannot carry bone scale: that is `limbscale` below.
- **`mv call posecheck '{"sheet":"zg_neutral"}'`** (`Editor/Native/PoseCheck.cs`, about 12 s for a 12 s clip) derives the clip, then plays it on a DontSave copy of HIM with his Animator and Avatar (the native way, no Foot IK) and writes `Director/library/poses/check/<clip>.png`: a row per key at its settled time, front | side | three-quarter | black silhouette (`"silhouette":"front"` to change), all at one scale; `<clip>.mp4`, the clip at 24 fps, front | three | side; `<clip>.json`: each key's body angles and muscles, and the playback's worst bone step a frame, toe angle range and neck range. Options: `variant`, `derive:false`, `play:false`, `secs`, `views`, `w`/`h`, `tileW`/`tileH`, `bg`, `fov`, `frame` (framing pad), `label` (adds `_<label>` to the files), `maxKeys`, `clip` (check any clip by name, at `times`). Judge from the images and the mp4 at speed, never from the numbers alone.
- **The angles** (degrees, posecheck `keys[].angles`): `hipFlex`/`hipAbd` the thigh against the pelvis, `knee`/`elbow` 180 minus the inside angle, `anklePF` the foot's own forward against the shin past 90 (pointed is +), `toes` the toe bone against its rest, `shFlex`/`shAbd` the upper arm against the chest, `neckFlex` the head's pitch down against the chest, `waistFlex`, `fingerCurl` (the clip catalog's: about 50 relaxed, 90 a loose fist), `headPitchWorld`. His standing idle (`mx_breathing_idle`) reads hip -5, knee 5, ankle -4 (right; the left foot of that clip is turned out), shoulder abd 18, elbow 30, waist 8.
- **His muscle scale** (`call posemuscles`, PersonAvatar uses Unity's default limits, humanScale 1.04): Foot Up-Down is 50 deg a unit (0.5 = 21 deg pointed past standing, 1.0 = 46: the NBP ankle needs no Avatar change), Upper Leg Front-Back -90..50, Lower Leg and Forearm Stretch +-80, Arm Down-Up -60..100, Arm Front-Back +-100, Upper Leg In-Out +-60, Spine/Chest/Neck/Head +-40. Upper Leg Twist + turns the knees out and brings the feet in; Arm Twist + swings the forearms out and up, - brings the hands together in front. Finger Stretched: +1 is about 23 deg curl, 0 about 75, -1 a tight fist (166); `curl` maps 0..1 to +1..-1.
- **`zg_neutral`** (`library/poses/zg_neutral.json`, clips `zg_neutral` and `zg_neutral_a`..`_d`): the base float, a 12 s loop. Measured: hip flex 36/32, abd 17/14, knee 49/46, ankle 23 pointed, toes 0, neck down 13 (head 15 below level), shoulder flex 46/42, abd 30/31, elbow 87/95 (forearms float up and in, hands at chest height, apart), fingers about 62 (relaxed), waist 12. Breath every 4 s (chest, shoulders, arms, a 4 mm body rise), each limb drifting 3-4 deg on its own 6 s phase, the body turning 1-2 deg over 12 s; worst bone step 0.3 deg a frame. Variants: `a` (right arm higher, head turned, a knee deeper), `b` (forward-pitched chaser: more hip and knee, arms forward), `c` (elongated: straighter legs, arms lower, head up a little), `d` (legs scissored, spine twisted, head tilted); each on its own cycle phase, so two copies never mirror. Check: `library/poses/check/zg_neutral*.png|mp4`, proof `zg_neutral_proof.mp4`.
- **`limbscale`** (`Runtime/MVLimbScale.cs`): `{"do":"limbscale","on":"person","bone":"rightHand","scale":1.5,"for":"0.25s","in":0.04,"out":0.12}` scales a bone (and what hangs from it) after the Animator, in the MVLate pass on a native body and last on a legacy one: up over `in` s, held, back over `out` s, inside `for`. `bones` takes a list at `scale` or a map `{"rightHand":1.6,"rightLowerArm":1.2}`; `scale` may be `[x,y,z]` in the bone's axes; cues multiply. Off by default: an actor with no `limbscale` cue never has a scale touched. For the strike frame toward the lens (1.2-1.6x, research 4.6): it is authored for one camera.
- **Rough for now:** the poses are muscles, so a hand can't be placed on a point (use `reach` on top, or IK later); interpolation is per muscle, so a 1s strike can swing a limb through the body: add a breakdown key and look; `overshoot` uses one settle shape; keyed clips are not in the clip catalog (lint may say `clip-uncatalogued`); posecheck has no floor and no shot camera (render the shot for that); `limbscale` assumes a bone's rest scale is 1 (true on him).

## Choreography

A dance take is composed first, as phrases on the beat grid, heard with the song and measured before any camera exists: `mv choreo build|play|check|segments <take>` (`Director/tools/mv/choreo.mjs`, format and the worked example in `Director/docs/choreography.md`). A take is `videos/<v>/choreo/<take>.yaml`, under 20 lines for 12 bars: each line a library phrase (`Director/library/choreo/phrases/*.yaml`: hook, groove, slide, turn, pose, walk, gesture) or a clip segment at a `bar:beat`, with `root` travel, `masks` for upper/lower mixes, `accents` (`pop` hip pops with overshoot and anticipation, `point` at the lens) and `word:baby` times from `lyrics.json`; a phrase with `performance` is a hook that repeats as one `perform`. `build` compiles to `library/performances/<take>.json` exactly as the hand-written Greener takes were (proof: `mv choreo test`, byte for byte, so the bake key and the bake stand), bakes it through `tools/bake.mjs`, and appends `videos/<v>/choreo/report.md`. `play` is a 720x480 mp4 of the lab, front and three-quarter, with the song slice mixed in (`--strip` every frame, a second a row): judge takes there at normal speed with sound. `check` prints accents per bar, worst gap, longest hold, beat landing (motion beats read off the body against the song's beats), travel and the bake numbers, and fails on `gates`. Greener's chorus is the first take and its phrases the first library entries.

## The score

`Director/score.json` plus `Director/acts/*.json` in name order. Comments allowed.

Inside a macro, `"+1b"` is measured from the macro's start, not from the previous cue. Times: `"12:3"` bar:beat, `"12"` bar, `"41.2s"`, `41.2` seconds, `"m3"` a mark, `"s5"` a section, plus an offset `"m3+2b"`, `"+1bar"`.
Lengths: `"2b"`, `"1bar"`, `"0.5s"`. Any cue: `at`, `for`, `until`. Repeat with `"repeat":"1b"` + `times`/`until`, or on hits with
`"onsets":"kick|snare|hat|all"` + `until`. `"macro":"name"` expands `macros`, `$key` substitutes.

- **Actors** `actors.<name>`: `model` (an alias from `models`, a path, or `prim:cube`), `pos rot scale color emission`, `from`/`until`,
  `crowd {count shape:grid|circle|line|scatter spacing radius seed modelScale scaleVar face}`, `models` to mix, `tv {...}`, `tvworld` (a surface that is a giant TV).
- **Shot** `{"at":..,"shot":{name pos rot fov move rotate look follow offset focus aperture handheld dutch dolly:[points] orbit:{around radius y rate angle} frame:[actors] noise blend fovTo fovFor fovEase}}`. `fovTo` is a crash zoom, or a dolly zoom with `move`.
  Runs until the next shot. Mostly cuts; `blend` only when creative. `look`/`focus` take bones: `person.head`, `.chest`, `.hips`.
- **Motion** `{"do":kind,"on":actor}`: holds after it ends: `set move rotate scale jump pop shrink spin orbit suck fall scatter tint glow show hide`.
  Only inside the clip: `hop bob sway shake pulse squash react`. `anim` plays `clip` (any clip in the project by name; `beats` locks a cycle to the beat, `offset`, `speed`).
  On crowds it acts per member: `stagger`, `jitter`. `every` is beats.
- **Acting** (actors with a Humanoid rig). All tween with `for`/`ease` and hold; only the bones you name are touched, over whatever clip plays.
  - `anim`: `{"do":"anim","on":"person","clip":"IdleSit14","blend":"0.3s"}` hands over from the last clip (default 0.2 s; 0 cuts): on a human by inertia, on anything else by a crossfade. See **Transitions**. `"once":1` plays once and holds the last frame (default loops); `"return":"0.4s"` blends back to the previous clip after `for`. `start`, `stop` (seconds into the clip), `speed`, `beats`, `offset`. Any clip in the project by name; `mv find <words> --type model`, then `mv pull "ULTIMATE ANIMATION COLLECTION" "Humanoid@<Name>.fbx"` brings one in. The turkey clips are `bite death eat getHit1 getHit2 idle jump prancing roll run runAngry sleep squart swim walk`.
  - `walk`: `{"do":"walk","on":"person","clip":"Sprint","to":[x,y,z],"for":"2bars","stride":3.0}` moves, turns to face the way, and ties the clip to the distance so feet don't skate. `stride` is metres per cycle (the clip's real one: Walk6 0.335, Walk4 0.66, measured from the stance foot); `turn`, `face:0`, `rel:1` (from where he is), `rel:2` (in his own frame). `"soft":0.5,"softOut":0.9` are seconds of speeding up and slowing down with a steady pace between. Cue the next `anim` when it arrives. Works on turkeys.
  - `pose`: `{"do":"pose","on":"person","bone":"rightUpperArm","rot":[x,y,z],"for":"1b","ease":"inout"}`, or `"bones":{"head":[..],"chest":[..]}`. `rot` is degrees added on top of the clip, in the actor's own frame, whatever the bone's axes: x tips toward his front, y turns to his right, z rolls toward his left. `[0,0,0]` eases it back. `"space":"local"` uses the bone's own axes. Bones: HumanBodyBones names (`head neck chest spine hips leftUpperArm leftLowerArm leftHand leftUpperLeg leftLowerLeg leftFoot`, same for right). On a turkey, any bone name.
  - `reach`: `{"do":"reach","on":"person","hand":"right","to":"remote","for":"1b"}` two-bone IK to an actor, bone (`"person.neck"`) or `[x,y,z]`; `offset`, `hint` (where the elbow points), `handRot`. `"weight":0` releases. `"foot":"left"` instead of `hand` does a leg.
  - `gaze`: `{"do":"gaze","on":"person","to":"tv1.screen","for":"1b"}` chest, neck and head share the turn and the eyes get there first (`lead`, 2.6 times sooner). `"weight":0` releases, `limit` degrees. `"head":0` moves only the eyes. `"toRel":[x,y,z]` is a point in his own frame instead of `to`.
  - `fingers`: `{"do":"fingers","on":"person","hand":"right","curl":0.8,"for":"0.2b"}`; `"finger":"thumb|index|middle|ring|pinky"` for one. `curl` adds to what the clip does. To set a hand outright, whatever the clip does, use `"set"`: 0 flat, 0.1 open, 0.35 relaxed, 0.65 half shut, 1 a fist, with the little finger curling more than the index and the thumb brought in beside the palm. It holds and beats `curl` until `"release":1`.
  - `stance`: `{"do":"stance","on":"person","arms":1,"legs":1,"shift":-0.025,"narrow":0.05,"drop":0.012,"tilt":2,"sway":0.008,"period":5.2,"bones":{..},"for":"0.5s"}` is how he carries himself, whatever clip plays. It holds, and sits under `pose`, `gaze`, `reach` and `plant`, so a shot's acting never replaces it. `arms` 0-1 scales `bones` (degrees, as `pose`). `legs` 0-1 scales the weight shift: hips `shift` metres to the side and `drop` down, rolled `tilt` degrees with the spine taking it back, swaying `sway` metres over `period` seconds on the scene's clock, feet held where the clip has them, `narrow` metres nearer the middle. Each key tweens alone: a walk cues `"legs":0` and keeps the arms. The `stand` performance sets it; `walk_*` turn off the legs, `kneel_down`, `sit_on_grass`, `lie_back` turn it all off, the `rise_*` turn it back on.
  - `eyes`: `{"do":"eyes","on":"person","yaw":6,"pitch":-2,"for":"0.07s"}` degrees on top of wherever the gaze left the eyes, in the head's frame, yaw + to his right, pitch + up. A dart needs no target and never moves the head, unlike a `gaze` with `"head":0`, which eases a head gaze back.
  - `plant`: `{"do":"plant","on":"person","foot":"both","for":"2b"}` pins the feet where they were at `at`, or at `pos`. Stays until a `plant` with `"weight":0` or a `release`. Hands and moving things: see Contact below.
  - `face`: `{"do":"face","on":"person","smile":1,"eyesWide":0.6,"for":"2b"}` shape keys `blink eyesWide smile fear jawOpen`. `fear` at 0.1 lifts the brow a little and reads as soft, not scared; `smile` over about 0.4 parts the lips in a close-up; `{"do":"blink","on":"person","for":"0.2s"}` once.
- **Perform** `{"do":"perform","on":"person","name":"walk_tired","for":"2bars"}` plays a tested, named action from `Director/library/performances/<name>.json`, expanded to cues at compile time; other keys are its parameters. Use these first. Only what `library/performances/gallery.md` lists as PASS is proven. Grips: `Director/library/grips.json`. How they are made and tested: `library/performances/README.md`, `node Director/tools/perf_lab.mjs <name>`.
- **Performance** (the animation engine: `Runtime/MVPerform.cs`, `MVOverlap.cs`; all pure functions of time). Prefer these over sliding him with `move`/`rotate`.
  - **Try it without building:** `mv lab file.json` plays cues on a throwaway copy of an actor and writes a sheet, no build, no scene change: `{"on":"person","from":0,"to":4,"every":3,"cols":8,"cues":[{"at":0,"do":"anim","on":"person","clip":"IdleBreathe","blend":0},...]}`, times as in the score. `"actors":{"person":{"model":"person"},"t":{"model":"p3_turkey","pos":[1,0,0]}}` for several (an actor of the open scene is cloned; `prim:sphere`, an `Assets/` path or an alias from the score's `models` stands in when it isn't, with `pos`, `rot`, `scale`, `color`); a video with no built scene yet says "run mv build once" instead of throwing; `"probe":[times],"probeBones":["hips","t.neck"]` prints positions. `"render":{"out":"library/x/name","views":["side","front","three"],"w":480,"h":640,"close":1,"focus":"rightHand","dist":0.8}` also writes real shaded 30 fps mp4s of the copy on a plain lawn, shape keys included (stand it far off with `"pos":[500,0,500]`). `"probeFace":t` lists its shape keys at t. It also proves purity: `reverseWorstMetres` and `shuffledFreshWorstMetres` must be 0. About 0.00003 at `"pos":[500,0,500]` is float rounding that far out, not a fault.
  - **Review:** `mv motion <from> <to> --every 2 [--on person --view side|front|back|top --cols 8 --file motion.png]` writes `Director/captures/motion.png`: every Nth frame at 30 fps as a flat silhouette from a fixed view (side = he faces left to right as he stands at `from`), blue line the hips path, yellow the hips now, green left and red right for feet and hands, a floor line, and a big panel below with all the ghosts and paths. It prints `pops` (a bone whose path kinks), `feet` (metres a foot moved while on the floor), `hipsTop`, `hipsLow`, `hipsTravel`. A bad arc, a pop, a foot slide show up here without a render.
  - **Motion check:** `mv motioncheck <from> <to> [--every 0.1 --on person,p4_grab --budget 60 --checks joint]` (or `call motioncheck '{"from":0,"to":204,"every":0.1}'`) walks the timeline without rendering and writes `Director/captures/motioncheck.json` and `motioncheck-report.md`: time ranges per shot, ranked by what the shot's camera sees, closer and larger first (in the frustum and not wholly behind something). It covers every humanoid actor plus hero turkeys. **joint**: elbow or knee past 150 or bent backward, shoulder behind 60 / across 50 / twist 120, wrist 70, ankle 65, spine, neck and head 45 per joint, head turn 80, nod 75, hip forward 135 / back 35 / out 60 / across 30 / twist 70, collarbone 45, each read against the parent bone from the rest pose. **overreach**: a bone longer than it was, or a `reach` target beyond the arm. **prop**: capsules between bones against temporary non-convex MeshColliders on each visible mesh (`Physics.ComputePenetration`, plus rays for a part wholly inside a closed mesh); crowds and other skinned meshes are a box at 70% of bounds (`how: bounds`). **self**, **bodies**: capsules against capsules. **floor**, **float**: feet and hips against the surface under them. **footslide**, **pop-rot**, **pop-move**, **bodyslide** run on all 30 fps frames, not across cuts and not during `steps`. **hand**: a finished `reach` or an `attach` over 3 cm off. The rest run every `every` seconds; 2 cm is ignored as contact. Attached items are ignored, `stretch` and `suck` skip a body, `--checks joint` runs only joints and overreach, much faster, and `--checks shot` only the camera and staging checks (Toolchain, "Shot checks"). Between samples only the bodies on camera are evaluated, so a body that comes on camera mid-sample starts at the next sample. It changes nothing in the scene (colliders are hidden DontSave objects, each result says `sceneDirtied`) and puts time back. Each piece takes its own turn in the queue; pieces stop at `--budget` seconds. Ragdoll and hard-falling poses read as contorted by design: look before fixing. `call motioncheck-selftest` proves the collider path.
  - **Root motion:** `{"do":"anim","on":"person","clip":"LandingHeavy","once":1,"root":1,"blend":"0.1s"}` plays the clip's real travel from where he is and facing as he faces: `_RM` clips, jumps, falls. It stops halfway through the next clip's blend and he stays where it took him. `"root":0.5` scales it, `"root":"y"` only height, `"root":"xz"` no height. **The clip's height comes with it:** `LandingHeavy` starts 0.53 m in the air and drops, so start him 0.53 up or use `"root":"xz"`. Most in-place clips (kneel, sit, get up) already move the hips and need no `root`. `call clipprobe '{"clips":["Name"]}'` shows what a clip's root and hips do.
  - **Keys:** `{"do":"keys","on":"person","at":"25:1","keys":[{"t":"1b","pose":"crouch"},{"t":"2b","from":"Idle1Wow@0.8","hold":"0.5b","root":{"pos":[0,0,0.6]}},{"t":"3b","bones":{"chest":[25,0,0]},"root":{"pos":[0,0,0.6],"rot":[0,40,0]}},{"t":"4b","pose":"kneel","ease":"out"}],"release":"0.5s"}` animates the whole body pose to pose, with a spline through the keys. A key is `t` (from `at`; `"25:3"` works too) plus any of:
    - `pose`: a name from `Director/poses.json` (a text library, add to it: `from`, `bones`, `hips`, `pose` to build on another). Starters: `stand crouch kneel kneel_slump kneel_hands_up sit sit_floor lay dead_back hands_up cower stunned hit_front yell surprised wow land_low crawl fall_air recoil lean_in slump`.
    - `from`: `"Clip@0.4"` takes that frame (seconds; `"Clip@40%"`) of any clip in the project, whole body. Find strong frames with `mv lab` and `"every":1`. Clips are in `Director/assets/motions.md`.
    - `bones`: degrees on top, same axes as `pose`. With no `pose`/`from` a key is what plays underneath plus these.
    - `hips`: `[x,y,z]` metres in his frame. `root`: `{"pos":..,"rot":..}` moves the whole actor, relative to where he stood at the start of the cue, in his frame; it stays after the keys end, and a key without `root` keeps the last.
    - `hold`: stays on this pose that long before moving on. `ease`: shapes the move arriving at this key (`in out inout linear back elastic bounce`), otherwise the spline. `flat`: 1 stops dead on this key.
    The first key at `t` above 0 starts from what plays underneath; a first key at 0 blends in over `blend` (default 0.2 s, `0` on a cut). After the last key he holds until `release` runs out or the next `anim`/`walk` starts (it fades out over that clip's `blend`). Keys sit on top of clips and under `pose`, `gaze`, `reach`, `plant`. `{"do":"pose","on":"person","pose":"cower","for":"1b"}` or `"from":"Clip@0.4"` is one key at `for`. Use `plant` for the feet when the body moves over them: keys don't know the floor.
  - **Layers**: `{"do":"layer","on":"person","at":"25:1","clip":"Cheer3","mask":"upper","weight":0.7,"for":"2b","blend":"0.3s"}` plays a clip over part of the body on top of the main clip, so one clip drives his legs while another drives his arms and chest. `mask`: `upper lower arms leftArm rightArm head spine`, or several: `"leftArm,head"`. `upper` is spine, arms and head; `lower` is hips and legs. On a turkey, words in bone names. `weight` 0-1, or keyed: `"weight":[["0b",0],["1b",0.8],["3b",0.2]]`. It fades in over `blend` and, after `for`, out over `blend`. Without `for` it holds until the next `layer` on the same mask, which crossfades over its own `blend`. `"additive":1` adds what the clip does on top of everything under it, measured from the clip's pose at `ref` (seconds, default 0): `{"do":"layer","on":"person","clip":"IdleBreathe","additive":1,"mask":"upper","weight":1}` breathes over a held pose or a ragdoll's last frame. Clip options as `anim`: `speed`, `beats`, `offset`, `start`, `once`. Layers go over clips and under `keys`, `pose`, `gaze`, `reach`, `plant`, in cue order.
  - **Blend tree**: `{"do":"anim","on":"person","blendTree":[["WalkBackwardsScared",0.6],["ScaredHostage",0.4]],"blend":"0.3s"}` mixes clips over the whole body, every clip at the same fraction of itself, so cycles of different lengths stay in step. The cycle is the weighted average length, or `"cycle":"2b"`, or `"beats":2`. `"mix":[["0b",[1,0]],["2b",[0.3,0.7]]]` keys the mix over time (weights are normalized). It crossfades in and out like any `anim`, with `once`, `offset`, `speed`, `return`; `"root":1` adds up each clip's travel in its share as the mix changes.
  - **Weight** on `keys` (for every key, or on one key for the move arriving at it) and on bone `pose`: `"anticipate":0.15` is a small move the opposite way first, as a share of the move, starting `lead` (default 0.16 s) before `at`, so the move itself still starts on `at`. `"overshoot":0.12` swings past the end and comes back, `"settle":"0.4s"` is how long that takes to die (alone it gives 0.1 of overshoot). `"successive":true` (or a length per step, default 0.035 s): hips lead, then chest, neck and head, then arms, hands, fingers. Legs stay with the hips. On a turkey the delay grows down each chain. `{"do":"keys","on":"person","at":"25:1","anticipate":0.2,"overshoot":0.14,"settle":"0.5s","successive":true,"keys":[{"t":0,"pose":"stand"},{"t":"1b","pose":"recoil"}]}`.
  - **Overlap**: `{"at":0,"do":"overlap","on":"person","amount":1,"for":"0.1s"}` on any rigged actor: head, arms and hands lag and swing from how the body really moved, including `move`, `root` and being carried. A spring on each limb tip driven by the body's own motion sampled back in time, so it matches backward. It holds; `"amount":0` fades it out over `for`. `head`, `arms`, `hands`, `torso` (0-1) scale a group, `freq` and `damp` change the springs. A cut or teleport is ignored. Hands in `reach` or `plant` stay exact. Costs about 1.5 ms a frame. On a turkey: `"chains":"neck,tail"`, words in bone names. It is off until cued; turn it on for a whole part when you want him alive, and lower it on a hard `move` (2 m in half a second swings the hand 25 cm). No hair: he has no hair bones.
  - **Contact**: `plant` also takes hands and a moving place. `{"do":"plant","on":"person","hand":"right","to":"sofa","blend":"0.1s","for":"4b","release":"0.3s"}` locks the hand on the sofa, on the spot it was on at `at`, and it stays there as the sofa moves, keeping its turn on it (`"lockRot":0` to let it turn). `to` takes an actor, a bone (`"p3_turkey.neck"`) or `[x,y,z]`; `"offset":[x,y,z]` picks the spot on the thing instead, and the hand goes there over `blend`, so reach with `reach` first. If the arm can't reach, the body follows: the spine bends, then the hips are dragged (`"follow":0.5` less, `0` never; hands 1, feet 0). `release` lets go after `for`; without it, it holds until a `plant` with `"weight":0`. `"foot":"left"` does the same for a foot, `"hint"` is where the elbow or knee points.
  - **Joint limits** are on for every human, always the last step: after clips, `pose`, `keys`, `reach`, `plant`, overlap and ragdoll, each joint is eased back inside what a person can do, with a soft stop, so nothing bends the wrong way. Mocap stays as it was unless it was past a limit. A deliberately cartoon moment: `"limits":0` on any cue turns them off while it runs (`pose`, `keys`, `anim`...), `{"do":"limits","on":"person","amount":0,"for":"1b"}` turns them off until `"amount":1`, and `stretch` turns them off by itself. If he stops short of a pose you keyed, that pose was past a joint; `mv lab` prints `limitsMovedDegrees`, the worst correction per bone.
  - **Ground** is on for every human (`Runtime/MVGround.cs`), after clips, stance and overlap and before contacts and joint limits, from the finished pose alone, so it is pure of time. The lower foot sets the hips, then they sink `sink` metres (.045) so the knees bend. A foot near the floor is put on it, flat, by leg IK; a foot clearly lifted stays as the clip has it, blended by height; ankles on the floor stay `width` metres apart (.18) across his hips. So never lift a take per move or pose the feet flat. The floor is the height of his last `set`, `move` or `walk`, so a mark on a stair or a table top just works; a climbing `walk` tilts it along the slope; `jump`, `hop`, root motion and a `keys` root leave it behind, so he leaves the floor. `{"do":"ground","on":"person","floor":0.77,"for":0}` gives a world height until his next mark, `{"do":"ground","on":"person","amount":0,"for":"0.2s"}` turns it off, `sink` and `width` tween on the same cue. Off by itself in a ragdoll, `stretch`, while attached, and when the hips aren't over the feet or a knee is near the floor (sits, kneels, lying). Clip admission measures with it off. It changes every human in every video, so an old video looks different if re-rendered.
  - **IK:** elbows point back and out and knees forward when the limb is nearly straight, so they never flip; `hint` overrides. A hand reaching past arm's length leans the torso toward it, up to 30 degrees (`"lean":0` to stop that), then stops short. A `plant` hand drags the body (`follow`).
  - **Turkeys:** `keys` work by bone name on any rig (`"bones":{"neck":[0,0,50]}`, `root`, `from` with their clips, `successive`), overlap with `chains`.
  - **Ragdoll**: `{"do":"ragdoll","on":"person","at":"25:1","for":"3b","impulse":[0,1,-2.2],"blendIn":"0.08s","seed":3}` is physics baked at build time into a clip in `Assets/MusicVideo/Generated/Ragdoll/`, from his pose and speed at that moment, so it plays and scrubs like any clip, same seed gives the same clip, and only a changed cue or pose bakes again. `impulse` is metres per second added to the whole body (`"hit":"chest"` to one bone, harder), `spin` degrees per second, `floor` the floor's world height (default: under his feet at `at`, so give it when he starts in the air), `"collide":["sofa","wall_n1"]` actors that stop him, boxes around their meshes where they stand at `at`; they don't move, `gravity` `[x,y,z]`, `drag`, `stiff` (joint damping, default 1.6). `drive` 0-1 holds toward his pose at `at`, or toward `"pose":"cower"` / `"from":"Clip@0.4"`: 0 limp, 0.1-0.3 he fights it, 0.6 a board. `"thrash":40` makes what he fights for wander by that many degrees (`thrashRate` 3.5), with `seed`: struggling. He holds the last frame until the next `anim`, which picks up where he fell (the actor moves there as that clip blends in, over its `blend`; give it a long one, or a get-up clip); he keeps facing as he did before the fall. `"blendOut":"0.3s"` instead fades back to what played before, where he stood, after `for`. His own body parts don't collide with each other. Try it in `mv lab` first: `"actors":{"person":{"model":"person"},"sofa":{"model":"sofa","pos":[0,0,-0.95]}}`, `"collide":["sofa"]`.
  - **Transitions** on humans are inertialized (`Runtime/MVInertia.cs`), for `anim`, `walk`, blend trees, `return`, and `keys` coming in and being released. The new source is a hard cut underneath: it is the truth at once. What the cut would jump by, in pose and in speed, is measured from the score at the moment of the change, by sampling both sides, and dies on a critically damped curve. `blend` keeps its meaning and its timing: half gone at half of `blend`, as the crossfade was, 85% at `blend`, gone at twice it. So old acts keep their timing. `"crossfade":1` on the cue asks for the old blend, for a slow dissolve between very different poses. `layer` weights still fade, and a turkey's clips crossfade. A clip after a ragdoll crossfades by itself, because the actor walks to the hips at the blend's own rate. Offsets stack without history: each is measured from the score alone, so interrupting a transition needs nothing kept from the last frame.
  - **Steps per actor:** `{"do":"steps","on":"person","rate":12,"for":"4bars"}` shows the whole actor at a quantized time: bones, position, face, whatever is attached to him. `rate` is poses a second, counted from `at`. Camera, particles and the world stay on ones. Bone groups, with the mask names from `layer`: `"mask":"arms"` steps only those bones and leaves the rest on ones, `"bones":{"arms":8,"head":6}` gives groups their own rates over `rate` (0 is on ones). Each group costs a whole extra body evaluation. On a camera it does nothing. Motion check skips kinematics for a stepped actor, as it does for world `steps`. Renders are 30 fps, so `15` is true twos, `10` threes; `12` lands unevenly, 2 and 3 frames in turn. A global `style` `steps` is the world version of this.
    Honest note, from one test (bars 25-26, the sit and lean back, in the lab; `node Director/library/proof/steps.mjs`, `captures/proof/steps_compare.png`): of 83 frames, 14 held still on ones, 43 at 15, 51 at 12, 12 with arms at 8, so the steps land as asked. Nobody has watched it yet, so "looks better" is not established. That move is slow, so most of what changes is frames that repeat, which is the case the research warns reads as dropped frames: use it where poses are strong and held (`keys` with `hold`) and moves are fast, leave a slow drift on ones. `mv lab` pops went 7, 12, 18 and will fire on any stepped actor: that is the jump itself, not a fault. Not tested yet: a camera with `look` on a stepped bone steps too unless the dead zone swallows it, so check a real shot, and motion blur on a stepped actor (the research says turn it off).
  - **Beat-warp:** `{"do":"anim","on":"person","clip":"Cheer4","warpTo":"kick","for":"4bars"}` (also on `layer`) lands the clip's motion beats on song hits. A motion beat is a sharp dip in how fast hands, feet, head and hips move: the moment a body stops and turns. At build the compiler picks which beats go on which hits, by dynamic programming, and bakes a curve of clip time into the cue; playing it is a lookup, so it scrubs and reverses. `warpTo`: `kick snare hat all beat bar`. `maxRate` (default 1.5) is the fastest or slowest the clip may run against its own speed, `warpStiff` (default 1.2) is how much it prefers its own speed over catching a hit. `speed`, `beats`, `offset`, `start`, `once` and `root` all still apply. Not every beat lands: it only takes the ones it can reach. `mv lab` prints `warp` with the pairs it chose, and `"motionBeats":1` prints the beats read off the body as it really moved.
  - **Baked takes:** `node Director/tools/bake.mjs <take...> [--video greener] [--clips] [--report]` (no take: every dance take) plays a finished take at 60 Hz with `call bakedump`, ground off, cleans it in Node (`tools/bake/clean.mjs`, `hands.mjs`) and writes a plain transform clip with `call bakewrite`: `Assets/MusicVideo/Generated/Baked/bk_<key>.anim`, `library/performances/baked/<key>.json`. The `perform` line doesn't change: the compiler finds the bake and plays it in place of the take's body. The source stays the source: the key hashes the take, what it nests, the parameters it reads, its time and length, so editing any of them makes the bake stale, the take plays from source and the build warns; run the tool again. Delete the `.json` to play from source on purpose. Each use of a take gets its own bake, because a walk's length and a warp's beats belong to where it sits. While a bake plays, ground, limits, overlap, stance and fingers stand down, fading over the clip's `blend` (0 on a cut). Its own `anim` and `walk` stay only as the actor's path (`rootOnly`), its `face` and `blink` stay, and `lead` is the clip's `start`. A card's `reach`, `fingers` and world `gaze` still go on top; a gaze there is a nudge of up to 32 degrees that fades out when the target is behind him, and the eyes do the rest. World `steps` step the clip as they step any clip. The cleaner: spikes out of every bone; chest and head held in a cone around up, with a neck limit; each foot built, not filtered: stances dead still on the floor, a clip's slide turned into a held foot and a short lifted step, heel up when the knee drives over the toes, heading near the hips, no snap of the ankle; knees and elbows true hinges with the axis from the skeleton, never the take; wrist path kept, elbow swing low-passed, twist carried by the forearm; no fingertip in front of his face; a relaxed hand with slow life, leaving a point or a fist alone. The last pass is `tools/bake/mechanics.mjs`, on joint angles so hinges stay hinges: the upper arm leads and the forearm and hand trail it on springs that overshoot a stop and settle, each reading ahead by its own lag so hits stay on the beat; shoulders against the hips, head ahead of a turn, a small drop as weight lands, a heel-toe roll on steps that travel. `TAKE` at the top of `bake.mjs` sets each take: `planted` for a seat or a stair, `sing` (moves with the phrase: breath timed to the lyric lines, a nod on stressed words, chest and shoulders from phrase to phrase, from `lyrics.json` and the vocal envelope), `sway` metres of weight shift, `talk` degrees a hand lifts. `--compare` renders with and without. A take must play a clip of its own: with none, a `keys` `hips` adds up in the dump and he sinks through the floor. A clone on the same take at the same time shares the bake. It works in the whole take at once, which a pure engine can't. `videos/<v>/bake-report.md` has the before and after, `captures/dance/bake/*.mp4` the side-by-sides. Purity: `reverseWorstMetres` and `shuffledFreshWorstMetres` are 0 on a baked take, and a clip is the reason.
  - **Clip admission:** a clip enters the library by playing on our rig. `node Director/tools/clip_admit.mjs <clip>...`, `--list Director/library/admit-list.json [--pull]`, `--mixamo`, `--report`, `--verdicts` (judges stored measurements again, no Unity, after changing `LIMITS` at the top of the tool). It runs the lab's body stack through `call admit` with root motion on and joint limits measuring, about 8 clips a second, and writes `Director/library/clips.json`: per clip `status` (`admitted` or `rejected`), `issues`, `notes`, `tags` (`idle sit kneel lay crouch walk run turn gesture reaction hostage injured hit transition`, `in-place travelling treadmill`, `on-floor low`, `loop one-shot`, `quiet high-energy`, `rhythmic` and more), `length`, `loop`, `loopGap`, `rootTravel`, `rootYaw`, `hipsTravel`, `motionBeats`, `tempoBeat`, `path`, and the raw `measured`. Checks: how far joint limits had to pull it back, pops, stance foot slide (against the clip's own speed when it walks in place), arms and legs into the body by capsules, feet under the floor. `node Director/library/select_clips.mjs` remakes the list. **Performances use admitted clips:** on a human, `anim`, `walk` and `layer` warn on every build for a clip that isn't in the catalogue or was rejected, with the reason. It warns, it does not stop the build. `keys` `from` frames are not checked: they take one pose, not the motion. Proofs: `Director/library/proof/`.
  - **The motion library** (`Director/library/motion/`, 2026-11-07): every Mixamo clip on our rig, watched at real speed, with what a choreographer needs. `gallery.md` has a contact strip and the notes per clip, by category (grooves, steps, turns, poses, walks, sits, lyric gestures, reactions, stairs); `clips.json` is the same in machine form: per clip `verdict` (A build on it, B a moment or a masked part, C don't), `style`, `segments` (`start`/`stop` clip seconds, `bars`, `what`, `use`), `masks` (the `layer` masks worth taking), `speed140`/`speed100` (the `speed` that puts the clip's pulse on the beat at those tempos), `avoid`, `tags`, and `performance` when a `g_*` performance was built from it. Use it before `videos/<v>/clip-picks.md`: pick a segment, play it with `start`/`stop` (or `warpTo`), mask what the notes say. `node Director/tools/motion_lib.mjs`: `render <clip...>` (under `mv hold`; real 30 fps mp4s, front and three-quarter, to `renders/`, plus the lab's numbers in `lab/`; 10-15 clips a hold, `mv refresh` between), `strips <clip...>` (ffmpeg: `strips/<clip>.jpg`, every frame at 15 fps in rows of one second, cropped tall so hands read; a clip over 8 s needs `--cell 240` or it is too small to grade), `gallery`, `todo` (mx_ clips with no notes). Notes are written by hand into `clips.json` after stepping the strip; a `?` verdict means rendered but not graded. The lyric gestures are performances: `g_point g_shrug g_hand_heart g_hand_cheek g_finger_lips g_wave g_bow g_hair_flick g_hands_up g_head_no g_nod`, each a layer over `dress_stand`, tested in `perf_lab` and baked (gate PASS). Admission is advisory for dance: most dance clips fail on stance foot slide of 3.5-5 cm/frame, which the bake removes. `mx_shrug` plays as a real shrug now (it imported as a T-pose before the clip import; the clip catalog checked it); Mixamo has no hair flick, curtsy or finger-to-lips by name.
  - **The clip catalog** (`Director/library/motion/catalog.md`, `catalog.html`, 2026-11-07): **stage from it.** Every humanoid clip in `Assets/MusicVideo/Motion` (180) plus the pack clips Hawkes Punch uses (9) has a preview on his rig through the NATIVE path (`previews/<clip>.mp4`, 640x360 30 fps, left a front three-quarter full body, right a medium on the upper body and hands; clean clip where used, the clip's Foot IK; up to 8 s, a longer clip shows its busiest 8 s), measures over the whole clip (`previews/<clip>.json`: hand curl and spread per hand, hands near the face or in the body, head yaw, forward lean, knee bent backward (advisory, many false alarms), drift, peak hand and foot time), and a verdict watched at 15 fps with 30 fps bursts (`verdicts.json`, brief in `verdicts/BRIEF.md`): `grade` A/B/C/ban, `uses` and `weak` by tag (`talk listen react laugh think shrug sigh gesture point wave thumbs-up clap bow write drink punch kick throw catch dodge hit-react fall taunt scared lift put-down pull look-back fly float sit kneel stand-idle walk sneak run turn stairs crawl injured dance pose jump transition`), `needs` (`relaxed grip`, a seat height, a start or stop time) and one line "good for; weak at; needs" with the hit or release time. Machine form: `catalog` in `Director/library/clips.json` (`tags` best first per use, `clips`). **`mv clips <use>`** prints the best clips for a use with their lines (`mv clips` lists the uses). **`mv lint`** warns on a native human (`clip-banned`, `clip-weak-use` when the card's idea, feel or note is about a use the clip is weak at, `clip-needs-grip` when a clip that holds the fingers flat or splayed plays with no `fingers` cue on that actor in the card, `clip-no-verdict`, `clip-no-preview`, `clip-uncatalogued`); legacy videos without `"native":true` are not checked. Tools: `node Director/mv.mjs hold node Director/tools/clip_catalog.mjs previews [clip...] [--force]` (Unity, `call clippreview` in `Editor/Native/MVClipPreview.cs`, about 5 s a clip, all 181 in 14 min; it skips clips that have both files), `clip_catalog.mjs strips [clip...]` (ffmpeg, `previews/strips/`), `build` (catalog.md, catalog.html, the clips.json section), `todo` (previewed, no verdict). After adding clips: clip import, clean clips, previews, watch and add verdicts, build. Found while grading: the `mx_turn_*` clips and `mx_walk_turn_180` import as loops with the turn baked into the pose, so looped they snap back each cycle: play them `once` with `root:1`; every walk previews in place (feet skate), judge walks with `walk`; `mx_hh_listening_2` is the same FBX as `mx_hh_listening`, and the six non-`mx_` clips are copies of their `mx_` twins.
  - **New clips:** Mixamo FBX go in `Assets/MusicVideo/Motion/Humanoid@<name>.fbx` (or `<name>.fbx`). They import as Humanoid with the root kept in the clip and the clip named after the file, so `"clip":"<name>"`, `"from":"<name>@0.4"` and `"root":1` just work. Use "Without Skin" downloads, in place or not. **Generated clips** (`gm_*`, 2026-11-07): `motion.from-text` runs through Gen on its RunPod 3D worker (HY-Motion for now, no finger motion, so every `gm_` clip needs a `fingers` grip). Ask for BVH only (no `character`, about $0.02 a job), then retarget in one `blender.character` job onto `Person/Person.fbx` with the bone map in `Director/gen/genmotion/GEN-BUGS.md` (his Unity limb names otherwise lose the arms and legs) and `from: smpl`; files and takes are in `Director/gen/genmotion/`.
    Prompting: "float" alone gives a kneel with the head thrown back to the sky. Write "upright, knees slightly bent, legs hanging loosely below, head level looking straight ahead" into the prompt and into every key pose (that took zg_idle from C to B). Big-body moves work; precise hand targets (face, shoulder, palms meeting) mostly fail.
  - **Native acting (new videos; 2026-11-07, `Director/review/engine-fidelity.md`).** A human on the native path is played by Unity's own Animator with its Humanoid Avatar, from Timeline AnimationTracks the compiler writes (`Editor/Native/NativeCompile.cs`): each `anim`/`walk` is a clip overlapping the last by its `blend` (a muscle-space crossfade with ease), `start`/`offset`/`speed`/`beats`/`once` map to clip-in, time scale and loop (`warpTo` plays at its average rate), a `layer` is an Override track with an AvatarMask for its mask (`upper lower arms leftArm rightArm head spine`, assets in `Assets/MusicVideo/Generated/Native/`). Our MotionTrack still moves the actor (marks, walks, root motion) and the face; after the Animator, in the same frame, it adds only `reach` (joint-rotation blend, elbow kept in the clip's own bend plane), `gaze` (neck and head only, `limit` 60, fades out behind him; eyes lead), `eyes`, and `fingers` (only the fingers a cue names: `set` for a grip). **Ignored on native actors, with a build note:** `keys`, `pose`, `plant`, `stance`, additive layers, ground, joint limits, the finger rest curl, overlap, inertial transitions, bakes (a take plays from source). Acting comes from clips: find one in `library/motion`, or make one with `call clipderive` (a clip plus keyed muscle offsets: `{"src":"IdleSit13","out":"sit_knee_kick_r","len":3,"add":{"Right Lower Leg Stretch":[[0,0],[0.17,0.75],[0.32,-0.12],[0.75,0]]}}`, muscle names as Unity's `HumanTrait.MuscleName`). On by default for videos made after 2026-11-07; `tkshock greener hawkespunch touchgrass demo _cards_test styletest` keep the old path. `"native":true|false` in a score or on an actor decides; `mv call buildnative '{"native":true,"fast":true}'` builds a video with every human native once, for a test, without changing the score. Rough: a `walk` plays its clip at the clip's own pace, not tied to the distance; a blend tree plays its heaviest clip; `steps` and `speed` don't reach native bodies; a layer at weight under 1 plays at full weight.
  - **Clip import and clean clips** (every humanoid clip, both paths). `mv call clipimport '{"apply":true}'` (`"prefix":"mx_"`, `"only":[..]`) measures each clip on his rig and sets Loop Time and Bake Into Pose on loops only, Root Y Based Upon Feet on all, XZ as root motion for travelling clips, and a per-clip Foot IK verdict (off unless it cuts slide by a third with no knee moved past 25 degrees); `Director/library/motion/import.json` holds the choices and the settings before. New FBX in `Motion/` get defaults once; `MVMotionImport` keeps what the `.meta` has after that. `mv call cleanclips` cleans each clip once in muscle space into `Assets/MusicVideo/Motion/Clean/<name>__clean.anim` (quaternion continuity, 1-2 frame flips out, a peak-keeping Savitzky-Golay pass on muscles, muscles inside the Avatar's ranges, feet held in the world through contacts where Foot IK doesn't flip a knee) and measures it against the source; a native actor plays the clean clip only where it beats the source on jitter, snaps and slide with peak speed kept within 15% (`clean.use` in `import.json`). Run both after adding clips. Fidelity tools: `call fidelity`, `call shotbones` + `node Director/tools/fidelity_shot.mjs old.json new.json`, `call evalorder`; side by side: `bash Director/tools/side_by_side.sh a.mp4 "A" b.mp4 "B" out.mp4`.
- **Attach**: `{"do":"attach","on":"remote","to":"person.rightHand","pos":[..],"rot":[..],"blend":"0.2s"}` follows a bone or actor until `{"do":"detach","on":"remote"}`, which keeps its world pose so `drop`/`move`/`jump` go on from there. `to` takes `actor`, `actor.bone`, `flock#3.head`. A person on a sofa: `attach` him to `sofa` and `topple` the sofa. On an actor: `"parent":"turkey_chef.head"` attaches from the start, `pos`/`rot` are the offset, `"scaled":1` scales with the host. Camera `look`/`focus`/`follow` and `fx` `on` take the same names, plus `person.mouth`, `.hand`, `.lefthand`, `.foot`.
- **Weight**: `topple` `{"do":"topple","on":"sofa","pivot":[0,0,-0.45],"axis":[1,0,0],"angle":-85,"bounce":0.25,"land":"25:3","at":"25:1","for":"1bar"}` rotates about an edge (`pivot` from the actor, or `"world":1`), falling under gravity and landing on `land`, then bouncing inside `for`. `drop` `{"do":"drop","on":"lamp","floor":0,"bounce":0.35,"height":1.2,"tumble":[80,0,20],"for":"2b"}`. `slide` `{"do":"slide","on":"table","to":[0,0,1.4],"hits":"kick","yaw":4,"for":"2bars"}` stick-slip: it jerks a bit on each hit inside `for` and stays still between (`dir`+`step` instead of `to`; `jerk`). `tremble` `{"do":"tremble","on":"glass","amount":0.006,"band":"kick","for":"2bars"}`. `wobble` `{"do":"wobble","on":"frame","amount":6,"decay":3}` a ringing swing after a hit. Soft props, which the compiler subdivides for you: `ripple` `{"do":"ripple","on":"carpet1","amount":0.1,"wave":0.8,"speed":1.5,"angle":0,"for":"2bars","fade":"0.3s"}` (`"pulse":1` for one hump crossing it), `peel` `{"do":"peel","on":"wall_w1","corner":[1,1,0],"to":1,"curl":0.3,"for":"1bar"}` (0..1 from that corner, holds; `side":-1` lifts the other way; send the rest off with `suck`), `bulge` `{"do":"bulge","on":"curtains","toward":[0,1,2.5],"amount":0.2,"radius":1.2,"for":"2b"}`.
  Rough for now: `walk` needs a following `anim` to stop cleanly. `peel` assumes a flat sheet and thin walls seen from behind can look thin. The stick-slip `slide` jerks only on hits, so give it `hits` that fall in its window.
- **Hawkes Punch mechanics** (2026-11-07, `Runtime/MVPunch.cs`, `MVPunchFx.cs`, `Editor/MVPunchScore.cs`; every one a pure function of time, proven forward and backward at 8 moments). Any of these on a human turns his ground pass off while it runs.
  - **Fly** `{"do":"fly","on":"person","points":[[600,1,0],[603,8,20],[600,30,60]],"fromHere":1,"for":"4s","ease":"in","face":1,"roll":180,"trail":{"lines":16}}` moves the actor root along a Catmull-Rom through `points` (`fromHere` prepends where he is, so no pop), or `"to":[x,y,z]` with `"arc":6` (a bump along `up`). Length is `for`, or `"speed":22` (m/s, needs `points`). It holds the end. `face:1` turns the body along the path, pitch included (`"face":"yaw"` keeps him level), blending in over `turn` (0.3 s); the bank into each curve is atan(v²κ/g) capped at `bank` (40°), `roll` degrees is a barrel roll over the flight, `tilt` [x,y,z] a fixed offset (lean back for superman). The body is yours: a held `pose` (`fall_air`), `keys`, a performance or a clip plays underneath. `trail` (1 or a map) adds an fx that follows the same path: a streak `width` (.35) over `length` (.22 of the path) and `lines` (14) speed lines in a `spread` (1.1 m), `color`, `alpha`, fading out over `fadeOut` (0.4 s). Trail is an `fx` too: `{"fx":"trail","on":"person",...}` with the fly's keys plus `flyStart`,`flyDur`.
  - **Throw and catch** `{"do":"throwobj","on":"can","from":"doctor.rightHand","to":"person.rightHand","for":"1.2s","arc":6,"spin":900,"catch":{"pos":[0,0.05,0.08],"squash":0.3}}`: the object leaves where `from` is at the cue's start (default: where the object is; `"from":"swing"` where its last `swing` left it) and lands where `to` will be at the cue's end, so a moving catcher still catches, on a parabola of `arc` metres (default 0.18 of the distance; `"straight":1`), spinning `spin` degrees about `axis`; `ease` (linear). `fromOffset`/`toOffset` [x,y,z] world, or in the bone's frame with `"rel":1`: a planet-sized actor sits beside the hand, not on it. `catch` attaches it on arrival (same sample, same time: no pop) with `pos`/`rot`/`blend`/`scaled` as `attach`, plus a `squash` (0.25) that decays over `squashFor` (half a beat). `{"do":"catch","on":"can","to":"person.rightHand","pos":[..],"squash":0.3}` is the same on its own. `detach` first if it was attached.
  - **Lasso, swing, throw** `{"do":"lasso","on":"person","to":"saturn","for":"1b","hold":"12s","hand":"right"}`: a rope fx from his hand (`thick` .035 m, `color`) whose loop flies out spinning open over `for`, tightens over `tight` (0.25 s) round the target (a great circle at its own radius; `radius` overrides, e.g. the rings), then stays `hold` (or `release` time). The rope sags by `sag` (.1) in flight and `sagTight` (.025) after, and its length follows the distance every frame. Then `{"do":"swing","on":"saturn","around":"person","turns":2,"for":"3s","lift":3,"ease":"in","radius":1,"axis":[0,1,0]}` orbits the roped thing round the mover from where it was when the swing began, `radius` scaling the distance, `lift` a rise and fall, `spin` on itself; it follows the mover after. Then `{"do":"throwobj","on":"saturn","from":"swing","to":"other.chest","toOffset":[0,0,-4],"for":"1.2s"}`. Direct: `{"fx":"rope","from":"person.rightHand","to":"saturn","fly":1,"for":"12s"}`.
  - **Impact** `{"at":"25:1","fx":"impact","on":"other.chest","size":5,"shake":0.4,"hitstop":3,"gravity":0,"color":"#ffcc70"}`: a flash held `flashFor` (0.07 s, 2 frames) at `flash` (0.5), a shockwave ring and a second ring, `count` (90) debris chunks (`debris` colour), sparks, dust and a point light, all scaled by `size` metres; `gravity` 0 in space. `shake` is a camera shake pulse for `shakeFor` (0.2 s), `hitstop` holds the picture that many frames and then picks up where the song is (2026-11-08: a hold in the warp, not a speed of 0, so nothing after it moves against the cuts; before, every hitstop frame put every later cast change a frame before its cut). It scrubs: a seek into or past a hold equals stepping through it. `resume` other than 1 still sets a speed after. `{"do":"hitstop","on":"person","frames":3}` is the pause alone. `"normal":[x,y,z]` faces the ring.
  - **Set break** `{"do":"shatter","on":"window","dir":[0,0.2,1],"count":180,"push":6}`: the prim hides and bursts into `count` glass shards from its own size (`size` overrides) along `dir` (`push` m/s, `speed`, `gravity` 1, `life` 2.5, `color`), with a glint and a flash. Roof pieces: a crowd of cubes and `scatter` (`power`, `lift`, `gravity`, `floor`), or `shatter` on each.
  - **Fold-out** `{"do":"fold","on":["backdrop","stool_l","stool_r","can"],"from":[0,0.5,70],"pivot":[0,-2,0],"axis":[1,0,0],"angle":90,"for":"0.6s","stagger":"0.25s","ease":"back"}`: each piece is hidden until its turn (`"appear":0` keeps it), then swings open on a hinge at `pivot` (actor-local, `"world":1`) from `angle` degrees about `axis` to its rest, growing from nothing at `from` (a world point; leave it out for a pure hinge). Several `on` go in order, `stagger` apart.
  - **Space camera**: a shot `{"follow":"person","offset":[2.5,1.2,-6],"lag":0.12,"lead":0.25,"look":"person.chest"}` rides the subject's path `lag` seconds back (a chase op on the camera's own track, no Cinemachine damping, so it scrubs) and aims `lead` seconds ahead of `look` (a `<shot>_lead` helper actor), `"space":"target"` turns `offset` with his heading, `"full"` with his whole rotation. The dead zone still bakes on the lead point; `"deadzone":false` turns it off. `"orbit":{"around":"saturn","radius":22,"y":6,"rate":20}` now follows the actor as it moves (a swung planet).
  - Rough for now: trail speed lines are straight along the path, not motion-blurred; a rope on a planet with rings needs `radius` by hand; `shatter` shards fall with gravity 1 unless told 0; the catch `pos` must match the throw's `toOffset` (`"rel":1`) or the object steps that far on the catch.
- **Epic toolkit** (2026-11-07, Hawkes Punch's space; `Runtime/MVEpicFx.cs`, `Runtime/MVHoldPose.cs`, `Editor/MVEpicScore.cs`, the end of `Shaders/MVStylize.shader`; the craft is `Director/research/epic-anime-action.md`). Every one a pure function of time and all off unless cued, so older videos render as before. **Frames** count at the score's `"fps"` (30 when it has none): `"frames":3`, or `"4f"` anywhere a length goes in these cues. Proof: `videos/_fx_test` (10 s, 24 fps, his rig; `library/sets/fx_void.json`).
  - **Impact frame** `{"do":"impactframe","on":"boulder","style":"flip","flash":1,"frames":2,"accent":"#ff3a2a","lines":1,"silence":4}`: `flash` frames of white (`flashColor`, `flashAlpha`), then `frames` (1-4) two-tone frames: `style` `black` (the subject black on white paper), `white` (the reverse) or `flip` (black-on-white, then white-on-black). The subject is everything nearer than `on`'s depth times `cut` (1.35), its own highlights left white, every silhouette inked, radial lines from it (`lines` 0 to drop), a rough edge; `"threshold":0.4` cuts on luminance instead. `accent` tints the paper. No `on`: luminance. Over 4 frames is refused (the flash ceiling). Plain look turns it off.
  - **Speed lines** `{"do":"speedlines","style":"radial","on":"person.rightHand","amount":0.8,"for":"0.5s","fadeIn":"0.1s","fadeOut":"0.2s","clear":0.22,"density":0.55,"width":0.14,"count":150,"ink":0,"hz":12}`: screen-space, drawn fresh every `1/hz` s (12: on 2s at 24 fps). `radial` converges on `on` (or `"center":[x,y,0]` on screen); `"style":"parallel","angle":20` streaks across the frame at that angle, clear round `on`. `ink:1` black lines, else white. Song time, so they keep going through a hitstop.
  - **Smear** `{"do":"smear","on":"person.rightHand","frames":1,"style":"both","length":0.5,"copies":3,"dir":[0,0,1]}`: on the strike's fastest frame, the limb (`rightHand`, `leftHand`, `rightFoot`, `leftFoot`: the chain above it) is drawn again from the posed mesh with his own materials, stretched back `length` metres along `dir` (default: down the forearm, as a punch drives) and/or as `copies` multiples back along it (`style` `stretch`, `multi`, `both`). The hand moves most, the shoulder not at all.
  - **Shockwave** `{"fx":"shock","on":"boulder","size":5,"for":"0.55s","width":0.07,"distort":0.6,"glow":0.25,"fill":0.12,"color":"#fff7ea"}`: a ring that tears out fast (cubic out: 70% of `size` metres in the first third), thin bright edge and a faint pressure disc, facing the camera or along `"normal":[x,y,z]`, plus a screen distortion (`distort`) and a glow on the ring's line on screen. Any size, metres to kilometres. On a sphere: `"sphere":"moon","angle":60` hugs the actor's surface from the hit point (`radius` overrides its bounds), growing over `angle` degrees: use `for` 1-3 s, slow reads as huge. On the space set's far layer: `"far":"jupiter","normal":[...]` (the axis in the body's frame) publishes the ring for MV/SpaceFar to draw (contract in `Director/gen/WORKLOG-epic.md`).
  - **Debris** `{"fx":"debris","on":"boulder","kind":"ice","size":3,"count":170,"speed":5,"normal":[0,0,1],"spread":80,"drag":0.35,"gravity":0,"spin":220,"dust":36,"for":"8s"}`: faceted chunks (four rock shapes, flat-shaded, lit by MV/Lit) flung from where `on` was at the cue's start: a few big slow ones, many small fast ones (`min`, `max` share of `size`), each spinning at its own constant rate, slowed by `drag`, no gravity unless asked; `kind` `ice` (pale, glossy) or rock (`color`, `color2`, `smooth`, `shadows:1`). `dust` puffs bloom, slow to a hang and stay (`dustColor`, `dustDrag`, `dustSpeed`), fading only at the end.
  - **Ice wake** `{"fx":"icewake","on":"other","for":"6s","emitFor":"1.5s","count":500,"size":2.2,"spread":9,"along":0.12,"radius":0.6,"drag":0.35,"dust":60}`: ice boulders blown out of the path of a flyer as he punches through a ring: his path over `emitFor` is sampled once (24 points), chunks leave it at their moment, outward across it and carried `along` with him, biggest up to `size` metres.
  - **Camera shake** `{"do":"shake","trauma":1,"punch":0.06,"for":"0.75s"}` (no `on`; `do: shake` with `on` is still the actor oscillator): Eiserloh's trauma, falling linearly over `for`, squared by the camera: roll up to 2.5 degrees and pitch and yaw worth 1.5% of the frame, smooth noise at 16 Hz seeded by song time (so it repeats exactly and runs on through a hitstop). `punch` narrows the lens that share for `punchFor` (0.18 s). `"heavy":0.8,"for":"2s"` is the slow 3 Hz sway of holding a planet (alone it lasts 1.6 s). Light 0.3, heavy 0.6, finisher 1.
  - **Hitstop that holds native bodies** (`do: hitstop` and `fx: impact`'s `hitstop`, now frames at the score's fps or a length `"0.25s"`): a native body now holds its pose through the hold as the world does. The compiler evaluates the timeline at each hold's start and keeps the bodies' poses (`MVHoldPose` on each native actor, put back before each camera renders), so the Animation tracks are untouched and every frame outside a hold is what it was. `"victim":"boulder"` shakes it across the hit (`axis`, default up and down as Sakurai has airborne bodies; `victimShake` 0.05 m, `victimHz` 20), decaying to nothing as the hold ends; `"attacker":"person","creep":0.03` creeps him forward. The hold is not seen by `motioncheck`/`shotcheck` (they never render).
  - **Freeze** `{"do":"freeze","on":"person","frames":14,"tremble":0.006}`: the anticipation hold. A native body's clip stands still, then goes on from where it stopped (its clips are cut at the freeze); `tremble` metres of root shake keeps the hold alive. Then a 2-4 frame strike: `{"do":"anim","clip":"mx_cross_punch","start":0.733,"speed":3,"blend":0}`. Put a freeze on a clip that is playing alone (a freeze inside a crossfade restarts it). `{"do":"hitshake","on":"x","amount":0.04,"hz":20,"for":"6f"}` is the shake alone.
  - **Two registers** on a card: `look: epic` (harder contrast, saturation, rim and sun rim up, a touch of chroma and halation, vignette) or `look: plain` (flat, soft, `faceFill` up, rim down; no speed lines and no impact frames). Added on top of the mood as look keys `epic` and `plain` (0-1), set with a 0-length grade on the card's first frame, so they switch on the cut and never blend; a card without `look` goes back to neither.
  - **Silence before a hit** `{"do":"silence","frames":4}` at the hit (or `for` from `at`): the whole mix (song, lines, sfx) gated to nothing for those frames, back on the hit's own frame (`videos/<v>/silence.json`, written by the build; `tools/mv/sound.mjs` mixes it for `mv shot` and `mv final`). `"silence":4` on `impactframe` or any `fx` does the same. Transport.cs's own mux (plain `mv render`) doesn't read it.
  - **Flash check** `node Director/tools/flashcheck.mjs <video.mp4> [--json f] [--quiet]`: Ofcom's rule on the file (a transition is 20 cd/m2 over a quarter of the screen with the darker side under 160; a run of steps one way is one; a flash is a pair of opposite ones): over 3 flashes in any second, or any saturated red flash, fails (exit 2), with times. `mv final` runs it on every finished file and writes `final/flashcheck.json`. Rule of thumb: an impact frame (flash, flip) is one or two flashes; keep impacts a second apart.
- **Other** `{"do":"fov","on":"feedcam","to":26,"for":"2b"}`. `{"grade":{"suckAt":"tv1.screen"}}` or `[x,y,z]` moves the warp's centre. `"fx":"dust","to":"tv1.screen","pull":0.3` drifts toward a point. `"model":"none"` is an empty actor for grouping.
- **Look** `{"grade":{...}}` tweens and holds; `{"pulse":{...},"every":"1b","sharp":2}` hits and decays. Keys: `bloom threshold chroma vignette distort exposure contrast saturation hue grain tint
  focus aperture motionblur shake dutch zoom handheld flash flashColor bars`, world warp `suck suckRadius twist wave toon`, screen `pixel poster halftone ink scan rgb swirl pinch radial tear invert kaleido blur duo duoA duoB mirror mask channel neon neonA neonB hatch`, library looks `vhs split splitA splitB lift halation paper flicker weave clay inkWidth aspect` (see Styles).
- **TV** `tv` (bright static feed tint mask), `tvflash`, `tvchange` (new picture and colour on the beat), `tvreact` (band, floor, gain). `tvchange` with `"keep":1` moves only the level, no new picture. `feed` shows the corner camera (`feedcam` is an actor).
- **Lights** `light`, `strobe`, `lightreact` (band, floor, amount), `sky {top mid bottom fog density ambient sun sunDir clouds stars ...}`. `"water":1.4` on `fx` `stream` draws bright unlit droplets instead of feathers. **Fx** `fx`: `explosion shockwave sparks feathers confetti fireworks smoke dust fire rain snow embers` or `prefab:Assets/...`, with `on`/`pos`.
- **Time** `{"speed":0.1,"for":"1bar"}` slow motion. `{"steps":12,"for":"8bars"}` animates the world on steps (stop motion); the camera stays smooth. Song time never changes; motion, animation and effects read scene time.
- **Text** `{"text":"..","style":"pop|slam|type|fade","size":..,"pos":[x,y,0]}`. **Sound** `{"sfx":"Assets/...","volume":1}`.
- **Inserts** `{"insert":"folder","for":..,"fps":16,"push":0.1,"loop":1}`: frames in `Assets/MusicVideo/Inserts/<folder>/`, picked by scene time.

- **Broadcast graphics** `{"gfx":"lower","title":"..","sub":"..","color":"#c01020","for":"4b"}` slides in and out; `{"gfx":"ticker","text":"..","label":"BREAKING","speed":260,"color":"#..","for":"8b"}` scrolls; `{"gfx":"bug","text":"WTKS 4","box":"#c01020","for":"8b"}` corner logo; `{"gfx":"phone","text":"CALL 1-800-GOBBLE","color":"#ffd000","for":"4b"}` throbs on the beat; `{"gfx":"channel","text":"CH 4","for":"2b"}` green number top right; `{"gfx":"image","path":"Assets/Shows/x.png","pos":[0.8,0.8,0],"size":[400,225,0],"for":"4b"}` any PNG (4 at once). Each lasts `for`. `"on":"feed"` draws it on the room TV's picture instead of the screen.
- **Channel flip** `{"flip":"CH 7","for":"0.5b","hold":"2b"}` (or `"flip":7`): snow over the whole frame for `for`, the green number stays for `hold`. Cut your shot on the same time or at the end of `for`. `"on":"tv1"` flips only that TV. Key `snow` also works in `grade` and `pulse`.
- **Texture on a prim** `"texture":"Assets/Shows/wall.png","tile":[2,1],"unlit":1.5`: `unlit` is the glow (so a screen shines in the dark), `color` tints it.
- **Shows** at the top level of any act: `"shows":{"news":{"set":"show_news","pos":[0,1.5,-3.5],"rot":[0,0,0],"fov":40,"channel":4,"lights":{"key":{"type":"spot","pos":[0,5.5,-5],"rot":[40,0,0],"intensity":3,"color":"#fff2e2","range":16,"angle":62,"shadows":true}}}}`. `pos`, `rot` and `lights` are relative to the `set` actor, or give `"cam":"<actor>"` to look through a camera actor you move yourself. The set's lights are on from the start, and `light` cues reach them as `news.key`.
- **On a TV** `{"at":"4:4.47","tv":"tv1","show":"news"}` cuts the picture to that show with a short snow flash (`"flip":"0.1b"`, 0 for none); `"show":null` is snow again. `feedcam` is the corner camera as an actor: `{"do":"set","on":"feedcam","pos":..,"rot":..}` and `{"do":"fov","on":"feedcam","to":26,"for":"2b","ease":"inout"}` still work when no show is on, and `"fov"` in a TV's `feed` block sets where it starts. A show's camera is `showcam_<name>`, and takes `do` cues like any actor.
- **Door** `"portal":{"offset":[40,0,6]}` on a prim quad turns it into an opening: a second camera, which is the main camera carried by `offset`, draws through it with real parallax, clipped at the far doorway. `"turn":180` turns the view about Y, `"res":[960,540]`, `"gain":1.2`, `"shadows":true`. The quad's +z must point the way you look through it (`rot` it). It costs a render only while the quad is visible, so give it `from`/`until`. A door never draws itself in its own view.
- **Sounds** `{"sfx":"tv_click","volume":0.8}`, or put `"sfx"` on any cue so it fires with it (every repeat and onset too): `{"at":"25:1","do":"move","on":"sofa",..,"sfx":"couch_slam"}`, with `"sfxAt":"+0.5b"` and `"sfxVolume":0.8`. A list picks a variant by time: `"sfx":["turkey_gobble_1","turkey_gobble_2","turkey_gobble_3"]`. A bare name is from `Assets/MusicVideo/Sfx/`, or use an `Assets/...` path. Sounds play in Unity and are mixed into `render`.
  Library (seconds): `tv_click` .2, `remote_thunk` .5, `tv_power_thump` .6, `channel_flip` .8, `static_burst` .2, `camera_servo` .3, `frame_tick` .2, `glass_ring` .4, `glass_stretch` 1.3, `couch_slam` .6, `wood_scrape` 1.1, `impact_big` 1.8, `turkey_gobble_1` .8, `turkey_gobble_2` .6, `turkey_gobble_3` 1.7, `turkey_flock` 2.7, `feather_whoosh` .7. More: `py Director/tools/sfx_make.py "<name>" "<description>" [seconds]`.

- **Rough for now (broadcast):** only one of each graphic per canvas at a time, so overlapping `gfx` of a kind: the last wins. Text on the feed is small at TV size, so keep it to a few big words. `flip` on the whole frame is a post effect, so `neon`, `swirl` and the like run before it. Doors do not recurse: a door seen through a door is blank. Sounds are shorter than asked for (`turkey_flock` 2.7 s) and nobody has listened to them yet.

Rough first versions from Engine FX, cost-limited on this laptop's GPU: keep one mountain on screen at a time; `"cards"` above 80000 can crash the GPU.
- **Feathers on him** `{"do":"feather","on":"person","amount":0.4,"from":"LeftHand,RightHand","for":"4bars","ease":"in"}`: `amount` 0-1 is the share of about 9000 feathers grown, tween it up, down or in steps. They spread from the `from` bones along his skeleton (hands, arms, neck, body, never the face). `"count":1` instead of `amount` grows exactly that many: the first is on the back of the first hand. `"strip":1,"to":[x,y,z]` (or `"to":"tv1.screen"`) tears them off in a stream to that point over the cue's length; `"strip":0` puts them back. Also `color`, `color2`, `size`, `lay`.
- **Mountain** an actor: `"p5_mountain":{"model":"none","mountain":{"radius":45,"height":22,"seed":5,"cards":240000,"holeW":5.4,"holeH":4},"pos":[0,0,-1],"from":"40:1","until":"56:1"}`. The summit is at `pos`, the base `height` below it. `{"do":"drain","on":"p5_mountain","to":1,"for":"6bars","ease":"linear"}`: 0 full, the summit dimples and a funnel bites down, the base reaches the bottom around 0.6, a rectangular hole (`holeW`, `holeH`, half sizes) opens from 0.45 to 0.72 so the TV shows through, and the rest sinks away by 1. Put the TV face up at `pos` minus `height`. His dent: `{"do":"dent","on":"p5_mountain","to":1,"x":0,"z":0,"radius":3,"depth":1.4,"for":"0.5b","ease":"out"}` (x, z from the summit).
- **Crown** `{"at":"41:1","fx":"crown","pos":[0,0.3,-1],"height":11,"radius":7,"count":3500,"floor":-1,"for":"60s"}`: a ring of feathers thrown up, then floating down. Runs on scene time, so `speed` slows it. `on` takes a bone. Also `size`, `drag`, `color`, `color2`.
- **Stream** `{"fx":"stream","on":"person.mouth","rate":500,"life":1.2,"spread":0.1,"for":"2bars"}` pours from a bone. `"to":[x,y,z]` or `"to":"tv1.screen"` goes to a point; without it they fall forward (`reach`, `drop`). `"pos"` instead of `on` is a fixed source, `"dir"` and `"push"` shape the curve, `"swirl"` twists it. The rope follows the source as it moves.
- **Stretch** `{"do":"stretch","on":"person","to":[x,y,z],"amount":1,"for":"2bars","ease":"linear","order":"feet,legs,hips,chest,arms,head,hand"}`: limb by limb he is drawn out to the point (or `"to":"tv1.screen"`) and thins into glowing scan lines. `hand` is the right hand, gone last; `arms` includes the left. Also `span` (how much limbs overlap, .3), `thin`, `lines`, `glow`. Camera `look` follows his bones, not the stretched skin.
- **Glass as skin** on a TV: `{"at":"28:4","do":"glass","on":"tv1","amount":1,"push":"p3_turkey.head","radius":0.14,"for":"0.1b"}` drapes the picture over whatever is pushing, in metres past the glass, tight over the ball and tented to the frame. `push` takes several, with radii: `"p3_turkey.head:0.12,p3_turkey.neck:0.09"`. `{"at":"29:1","do":"glass","on":"tv1","open":1,"for":"0.5b"}` tears it open around the first pusher, rings and stays open (`openRadius` 0.32, `tear`). `"amount":0` and `"open":0` make it plain again. The pusher must start behind the screen (z greater than the screen for `tv1`), inside the set.
- **Eye** an actor: `"eyeball":{"model":"none","eye":{"from":"person.head","to":"tv1.screen","skin":"#b27a60","iris":"#2b5566"},"pos":[-60,0,0]}`: a macro set facing +Z with lids, lashes and skin, ball radius 1, same place as the old prim, so old shots still frame it (`rot:[0,180,0]`, z 2.6 to 4.3). The reflection is a small camera really at `from` looking at `to`: whatever he is looking at is what lies in the tear film. `{"do":"eyesee","on":"eyeball","from":"person.head","to":[x,y,z]}` changes it. Controls: `{"do":"face","on":"eyeball","wide":1,"pupil":0.62,"squint":0,"lookX":0,"lookY":0,"reflect":1,"for":"0.25b"}`, `{"do":"blink","on":"eyeball","for":"0.3b"}`, or hold it shut with `"blink":1` in `face`. Grade keys `eyePupil`, `eyeTV` still work. Delete any light-only setup: the eye has its own TV-coloured light; `eye_key` adds a key.

Envelopes for `band`: `kick snare hat drums bass vocal other mid high rms bright`.

Type goes through the screen looks: `neon` turns it to outline, `radial` and `swirl` smear it. Keep lyrics on looks that leave it solid.

## Real-scale space (`space_epic`, 2026-11-08)

Hawkes Punch's space at true size (J: "make the planets actual planet size... think One Punch Man"; `videos/hawkespunch/SPACE-EPIC.md`). Set `library/sets/space_epic.json`, engine `Runtime/MVSpace.cs`, shaders `Runtime/Shaders/MVSpace*.shader` and `MVSpaceLib.hlsl`, maps `Assets/MusicVideo/Props/Space/` from `Director/tools/space_tex.py` (the Earth is drawn from hand-written continent outlines and ~230 cities; no downloads, no Gen spend). Proof video `videos/_space_epic` (not for J).
- **Two layers.** An actor with `"space":{"kind":...}` is one of: `root` (the far layer, the sun, the stars, the near lights, named `focuses`), `body` (a planet or moon: `look` earth|moon|jupiter|saturn|greymoon|rock, `radius` km, `atmo` km, `atmoColor`, `atmoStrength`, `tint`, `bright`, `albedo` and `bounce` colour for the light it throws on him, `day` seconds a turn, `haze` [r,g,b,km] and `hazeBelow` km for inside its air; Jupiter also `stormLat stormLon stormW stormH stormSpin wind`), `ring` (`body` that shades it, `inner outer` km, `profileFrom profileTo`, `opacity`, `nearFade` km, `clumps`, `spin` deg/s), `crater` (`body lat lon radius` km, `grow` share of the cue spent growing, `glow`), `speck` (a body too small to see, drawn as its light: `color glow size` px; a `planet_move` with `trail` s draws its streak), `field` (ring ice boulders: `ring radius count seed thickness sizeMin sizeMax hero:[[x,y,z,r]] clear:[[x,y,z,r]] color color2 ice smooth detail fadeFrom fadeTo`) and `surface` (`body`, `look` rock|cloud, `size` m, `planetRadius` m; rock: `craters craterMax rocks rockRadius rockMax clear`; cloud: `amp scale boil`).
  Bodies, rings, specks: their actor position minus the root's is **kilometres** (so `move`, `rotate`, `spin`, `jump` work on them in km). The far layer (`MV/SpaceFar`) ray-traces them per pixel along the near camera's own rays: the "far camera" has the near camera's rotation and lens exactly and its position is the near camera's divided by 1000, carried by the focus. Centres reach the shader relative to the camera, computed in double, with the altitude over the body you stand on exact, so a metre above Jupiter's clouds and a million km off both hold. It draws after the near opaque layer only where nothing near was drawn and before transparents, so characters, boulders and effects sit on top with their own depth. Fields and surfaces are real meshes in **metres** around the root (`MV/SpaceRock`, `MV/SpaceCloud`), and show only while the focus is on their body; the cloud deck takes its colour from the far layer's own Jupiter function, and both fade to the same `haze`.
- **The focus** says where the near world (the marks) is in the far one. Every card starts with one: `{at: 0, do: focus, on: space_epic_space, name: <focus>}`, a preset of the root's `focuses`, any key overridden on the cue: `body lat lon alt` (km; ground is y = 0, up is up), `ring r lon alt` (the ring plane is y = 0, +z outward), or `pos [km] rot`; `heading` turns the near world about its up, `rot [x,y,z]` tips it (`[90,0,0]` makes the planet a wall ahead, +z). With `for`, a focus glides: over the surface when both are on one body, else straight. The set's focuses: `arrival_streak arrival_rings ring_walk ring_chase ring_edge moon_ground moon_wide jupiter_bg jupiter_catch jupiter_low atlas earth_insert sunrise saturn_wide crater_wide`; marks for every beat of SPACE-EPIC.md are in the set with a note each.
- **Cues** (`MVSpace.cs SpaceStep`, reached from PunchStep): `focus` (above). `planet_move` on a body or speck: `to` [km from the root] or a body's name (+`offset`), `arc` km along `arcDir`, `ease`, `trail` s (specks). `planet_hold`: the body follows the near origin at `offset` [km, near axes] with `weight` (tweened by `for`): the Atlas hold is `{do: planet_hold, on: space_epic_earth, offset: [0, 6371.0023, 0]}` (its surface 2.3 m over his head), the catch-and-slide holds Jupiter while the focus is on it. `planet_release`: weight back to 0 over `for` (it eases back to where `planet_move`/`planet_orbit` put it). `planet_orbit`: back to where the set put it (`offset`, `arc`), on a smootherstep, "like setting down a full glass". `ring_hoop` on `space_epic_hoop` (the A ring, the main ring stops at the Cassini Division): `stage: lift` (`lift` km along its normal, `tilt` deg, `drift` [km]), `stage: lasso` (swings round `pivot` [km] or a body, `axis`, `turns`), `stage: throw` (`to` a body or [km], `arc`, `rot` the attitude it settles at: send it round Jupiter, inner radius 122,170 km against Jupiter's 69,911); `spin` deg/s turns it on itself from that cue on (shown by its clumps). `crater` on `space_epic_crater`: blooms over `for` (grows in the first `grow` share: a white flash, a molten floor and a glowing rim cooling to orange, the shock ring running out, then a dark fresh bowl with bright rays).
- **Light.** One hard sun from the root's `sun` direction (`sunColor sunIntensity sunSize sunDisc glare farSun`): MVSpace aims a directional with shadows at the near world per camera and makes it `RenderSettings.sun`, adds the **bounce** of the brightest planet in view (albedo x solid angle x lit fraction x `bounce`, capped by `bounceMax`, the body's `bounce` colour) and a **rim** from behind him on the side away from the sun (`rim rimColor rimUp rimSide`). Stars are fixed in the far frame, spread over at least a pixel so they never sparkle, dimmed by `starsLit` when a lit planet fills the frame. The sun's glare (`MV/SpaceGlare`: core, six-point star, anamorphic streak, ghosts) is drawn over everything and hidden by far bodies in front of the sun. The lighting preset `space_epic` is black sky and almost no ambient. **Hawkes Punch's score has a stage `key` light on all the time: turn it to 0 over space_epic cards** or it lights space from nowhere.
- **Scale rules that follow** (`research/epic-anime-action.md` 2 and 6): a man and a disc read together only at very different distances (him 50-200 m from the lens, the planet 10^5 km behind); contact shots are near-scale with the planet as terrain (a focus on the surface, a patch under him); a man too far to see is a `speck`.
- Rough: a focus glide between two bodies, or one started inside an unfinished glide, starts from the last focus's target; a patch stays under the near origin while the focus slides over its body (the far layer moves, the patch doesn't: hide the slide with speed lines or cut); far bodies don't cast shadows on the near layer (one sun, no eclipses of him); the near deck's billows are displaced in the vertex shader, so the motion check sees a flat disc there; a crater's near-scale dust and the impact are the effects agent's (`fx: impact`), the set gives the far crater only.

## How it works

Everything is a pure function of timeline position: `MVTrack`s fold over all their clips by absolute time, so any frame can be computed
on its own. That's why scrubbing and reverse work. Never add state that depends on the previous frame, and never use `Time.timeScale`.
Scene time comes from `SongMap.Warp` (`WarpRaw` skips `steps`, for cameras). A `ScriptableObject` type needs its own file named after it, or it won't save.

`Runtime/`: tracks and drivers, `Shaders/MVWarp.hlsl` injected into `MVLit.shader` (URP Lit, generated from the package copy), `MVStylize` the full-screen pass, `MVTvScreen`.
`Editor/`: `ScoreCompiler`, `Bridge` (file mailbox, `[BridgeCommand]`), `Transport` (the desk, capture, render), `Quality` (preview in Unity, final for captures and renders), `Setup`.

The song is 87 BPM, 73 bars, a 3-3-2 kick pattern. Re-run the analysis only if the audio changes: demucs into `Director/stems`, then `Director/tools/analyze_deep.py`.

## Rule from J: fully real-time 3D

No AI video or AI footage on screen. AI is allowed for textures, and for body motion data on our rigs (`motion.from-text`, J 2026-11-05). (The rejected AI shots are in `Director/acts_ai` and `Director/ai_footage`; don't bring them back.)
The person is a real rigged character: about 220k tris, 1.8 m, 53 bones with fingers and `LeftEye`/`RightEye` (Humanoid eye bones; the balls turn under the lids), shape keys `jawOpen blink eyesWide smile fear`. Since 2026-11-07 he wears low dark canvas sneakers on a pale sole (built in `blender_rig.py make_foot`, painted in `stage_paint`, part 2; the Greener wardrobe keeps them), his hands have knuckles, tendons and a soft wrist (judge them with `tools/person_hands.py`: relaxed, grip, spread, point, pinch, touch), the skin has pores with a smoothness that moves across the face, and the hair strip carries its own sheen (Head.mat reads `Person_Head_Gloss_hair.png`, which `person.sh` now writes). The previous model is in `Director/gen/rigged/backup-2026-11-07/`. `blink` closes fully onto a dark lash line. `smile` keeps the mouth shut: 0.3 to 0.7 is warm. `bash Director/tools/person.sh` rebuilds him (`--no-blender` only installs from `Director/gen/rigged/`). Turkeys: `call importturkeys`, props: `call importprops` copy from `Director/gen` with the right settings; aliases `turkey_<brown|black|white>` are the crowd model, `_hero` the close one; `tv` and `tv_light`, `tv_stand`, `camera`.
The eye is a real 3D eyeball (`MV/Eye`, keys `eyePupil`, `eyeTV`). `call bones '{"t":70}'` gives where his body is at a moment, for framing.
`render` runs inside Unity a few frames per tick and waits while J plays: `mv render --w 1280 --h 720 --fps 30 --wait`, `mv render-status`, `mv render-cancel`.

## Current direction

Read `Director/STORYBOARD.md` and `Director/POLISH.md` before working: every bar has an event, and work is judged against polished professional music videos.
